\id 2SA - Berean Standard Bible
\h 2 Samuel
\toc2 2 Samuel
\toc1 2 Samuel
\mt1 2 Samuel
\c 1
\s1 Saul’s Death Reported to David
\r (1 Samuel 31:1–6; 1 Chronicles 10:1–6)
\m
\v 1 \zdate|Q28730:P39:Q43459871:P582\*After the death of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* returned from the slaughter of the Amalekites and stayed in Ziklag two days.
\v 2 \zdate|Q28730:P39:Q43459871:P582+P3D\*On the third day a man with torn clothes and dust on his head arrived from \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s camp. When he came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he fell to the ground to pay him homage.
\b
\m
\v 3 “Where have you come from?” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked.
\b
\m “I have escaped from the Israelite camp,” he replied.
\b
\m
\v 4 “What was the outcome?” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked. “Please tell me.”
\b
\m “The troops fled from the battle,” he replied. “Many of them fell and died. And \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* are also dead.”
\b
\m
\v 5 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked the young man who had brought him the report, “How do you know that \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* are dead?”
\b
\m
\v 6 “I happened to be on Mount Gilboa,” he replied, “and there was \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, leaning on his spear, with the chariots and the cavalry closing in on him.
\v 7 When he turned around and saw me, he called out to me, and I answered, ‘Here I am!’
\b
\m
\v 8 ‘Who are you?’ he asked.
\b
\m So I told him, ‘I am an Amalekite.’
\b
\m
\v 9 Then he begged me, ‘Stand over me and kill me, for agony has seized me, but my life still lingers.’
\b
\m
\v 10 So I stood over him and killed him, because I knew that after he had fallen he could not survive. And I took the crown that was on his head and the band that was on his arm, and I have brought them here to \zwd my lord|Q41370\zwd*.”
\b
\m
\v 11 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took hold of his own clothes and tore them, and all the men who were with him did the same.
\v 12 They mourned and wept and fasted until evening for \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*, and for the people of the LORD and the house of Israel, because they had fallen by the sword.
\b
\m
\v 13 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the young man who had brought him the report, “Where are you from?”
\b
\m “I am the son of a foreigner,” he answered. “I am an Amalekite.”
\b
\m
\v 14 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked him, “Why were you not afraid to lift your hand to destroy \zwd the LORD’s anointed|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*?”
\v 15 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* summoned one of the young men and said, “Go, execute him!” So the young man struck him down, and he died.
\v 16 For \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had said to the Amalekite, “Your blood be on your own head because your own mouth has testified against you, saying, ‘I killed \zwd the LORD’s anointed|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*.’”
\s1 David’s Song for Saul and Jonathan
\b
\m
\v 17 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took up this lament for \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*,
\v 18 and he ordered that the sons of Judah be taught the Song of the Bow.\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Or be taught the use of the bow; literally be taught the bow\f* It is written in the Book of \zwd Jashar|\zwd*:\f + \fr 1:18 \ft Or the Book of the Upright One, commonly cited as Jasher; note that the Song of the Bow that follows is not found in known manuscripts attributed to Jasher.\f*
\b
\q1
\v 19 “Your glory, O Israel, lies slain on your heights.
\q2 How the mighty have fallen!
\b
\q1
\v 20 Tell it not in Gath;
\q2 proclaim it not in the streets of Ashkelon,
\q1 lest the daughters of the Philistines rejoice,
\q2 and the daughters of the uncircumcised exult.
\q1
\v 21 O mountains of Gilboa,
\q2 may you have no dew or rain,
\q2 no fields yielding offerings of grain.\f + \fr 1:21 \ft Or no showers falling on your terraced fields; LXX no fields of firstfruits; Hebrew no fields of offerings\f*
\q1 For there the shield of the mighty was defiled,
\q2 the shield of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, no longer anointed with oil.
\b
\q1
\v 22 From the blood of the slain,
\q2 from the fat of the mighty,
\q1 the bow of \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* did not retreat,
\q2 and the sword of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* did not return empty.
\q1
\v 23 \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*, beloved and delightful in life,
\q2 were not divided in death.
\q1 They were swifter than eagles;
\q2 they were stronger than lions.
\b
\q1
\v 24 O daughters of Israel,
\q2 weep for \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*,
\q1 who clothed you in scarlet and luxury,
\q2 who decked your garments with ornaments of gold.
\q1
\v 25 How the mighty have fallen in the thick of battle!
\q2 \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* lies slain on your heights.
\b
\q1
\v 26 I grieve for you, \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*, \zwd my brother|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*.
\q2 You were delightful to me;
\q1 your love to me was extraordinary,
\q2 surpassing the love of women.
\q1
\v 27 How the mighty have fallen,
\q2 and the weapons of war have perished!”
\c 2
\s1 David Anointed King of Judah
\m 
\v 1 Some time later, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD, “Should I go up to one of the towns of Judah?” 
\b
\m “Go up,” the LORD answered. 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Where should I go?” 
\b
\m “To Hebron,” replied the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went there with his two wives, \zwd Ahinoam of Jezreel|Q400266\zwd* and \zwd Abigail the widow of \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd* of Carmel|Q4416778\zwd*. 
\v 3 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* also took the men who were with him, each with his household, and they settled in the towns near Hebron. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then the men of Judah came to Hebron, and there they anointed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* king over the house of Judah. And they told \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “It was the men of Jabesh-gilead who buried \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent messengers to the men of Jabesh-gilead to tell them, “The LORD bless you, because you showed this kindness to \zwd Saul your lord|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* when you buried him. 
\v 6 Now may the LORD show you loving devotion \f + \fr 2:6 \ft Forms of the Hebrew chesed are translated here and in most cases throughout the Scriptures as loving devotion; the range of meaning includes love, goodness, kindness, faithfulness, and mercy, as well as loyalty to a covenant.\f* and faithfulness, and I will also show you the same favor because you have done this. 
\v 7 Now then, be strong and courageous, for though \zwd Saul your lord|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* is dead, the house of Judah has anointed me as their king.” 
\s1 Ish-bosheth Made King of Israel
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zdate|Q313238:P39:Q43459871:P580\*Meanwhile, \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd*, the commander of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s army, took \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s son Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd*,\f + \fr 2:8 \ft Ish-bosheth is also called Esh-baal; see 1 Chronicles 8:33.\f* moved him to Mahanaim, 
\v 9 and made him king over Gilead, Asher, Jezreel, Ephraim, and Benjamin—over all Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s son Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* was forty years old when he began to reign over Israel, and he reigned for two years. 
\b
\m The house of Judah, however, followed \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 11 And the length of time that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was king in Hebron over the house of Judah was seven years and six months. 
\s1 The Battle of Gibeon
\b
\m 
\v 12 One day \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd* and the servants of \zwd Ish-bosheth son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q313238\zwd* marched out from Mahanaim to Gibeon. 
\v 13 So \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd* and the servants of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* marched out and met them by the pool of Gibeon. And the two groups took up positions on opposite sides of the pool. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* said to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “Let us have the young men get up and compete before us.” 
\b
\m “Let them get up,” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So they got up and were counted off—twelve for Benjamin and \zwd Ish-bosheth son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q313238\zwd*, and twelve for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 16 Then each man grabbed his opponent by the head and thrust his sword into his opponent’s side, and they all fell together. So this place, which is in Gibeon, is called Helkath-hazzurim.\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Helkath-hazzurim means the Field of Swords.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 17 The battle that day was intense, and \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* and the men of Israel were defeated by the servants of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zwd The three sons of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1141064,Q2915076,Q2915076\zwd* were there: \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, and \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd*. Now \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* was fleet of foot like a wild gazelle, 
\v 19 and he chased \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, not turning to the right or to the left in his pursuit. 
\v 20 And \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* glanced back and said, “Is that you, \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “It is,” \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* told him, “Turn to your right or to your left, seize one of the young men, and take his equipment for yourself.” 
\b
\m But \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* would not stop chasing him. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Once again, \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* warned \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd*, “Stop chasing me. Why should I strike you to the ground? How could I show my face to \zwd your brother Joab|Q1141064\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 But \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* refused to turn away. So \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* thrust the butt of his spear into his stomach, and it came out his back, and he fell dead on the spot. And every man paused when he came to the place where \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* had fallen and died. 
\v 24 But \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* pursued \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*. By sunset, they had gone as far as the hill of Ammah opposite Giah on the way to the wilderness of Gibeon. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 The Benjamites rallied to \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, formed a single unit, and took their stand atop a hill. 
\v 26 Then \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* called out to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*: “Must the sword devour forever? Do you not realize that this will only end in bitterness? How long before you tell the troops to stop pursuing their brothers?” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 “As surely as God lives,” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied, “if you had not spoken up, the troops would have continued pursuing their brothers until morning.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* blew the ram’s horn, and all the troops stopped; they no longer pursued Israel or continued to fight. 
\v 29 And all that night \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* and his men marched through the Arabah. They crossed the Jordan, marched all morning,\f + \fr 2:29 \ft See LXX; Hebrew marched all through Bithron\f* and arrived at Mahanaim. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 When \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* returned from pursuing \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, he gathered all the troops. 
\b
\m In addition to \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd*, nineteen of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s servants were missing, 
\v 31 but they had struck down 360 Benjamites who were with \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*. 
\v 32 And they took \zwd Asahel|Q2915076\zwd* and buried him in \zwd his father|\zwd*’s tomb in Bethlehem. Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and his men marched all night and reached Hebron at daybreak. 
\c 3
\s1 The House of David Strengthened
\r (1 Chronicles 3:1–9)
\m 
\v 1 Now the war between the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and the house of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was protracted. And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* grew stronger and stronger, while the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* grew weaker and weaker. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And sons were born to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in Hebron: 
\b
\li1 His firstborn was \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd*, by \zwd Ahinoam of Jezreel|Q400266\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 
\v 3 his second was \zwd Chileab|Q2418113\zwd*,\f + \fr 3:3 \ft LXX Daluia; some translators Daniel; see 1 Chronicles 3:1.\f* by \zwd Abigail the widow of \zwd Nabal|id="Q1709507" t="2"\zwd* of Carmel|Q4416778\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 his third was \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, the son of \zwd Maacah daughter of \zwd King Talmai of Geshur|id="Q33026733" t="2"\zwd*|Q11765188\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 
\v 4 his fourth was \zwd Adonijah|Q360378\zwd*, the son of \zwd Haggith|Q5638717\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 his fifth was \zwd Shephatiah|Q9351247\zwd*, the son of \zwd Abital|Q33026741\zwd*; 
\b
\li1 
\v 5 and his sixth was \zwd Ithream|Q11727237\zwd*, by \zwd \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s wife Eglah|Q12630462\zwd*. 
\b
\m These sons were born to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in Hebron. 
\s1 Abner Joins David
\b
\m 
\v 6 During the war between the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and the house of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* had continued to strengthen his position in the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 7 Now \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* had a concubine named \zwd Rizpah|Q2899077\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Aiah|id="Q132154410" t="2"\zwd*. So \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* questioned \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, “Why did you sleep with \zwd \zwd my father|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s concubine|Q2899077\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* was furious over \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd*’s accusation. “Am I the head of a dog that belongs to Judah?” he asked. “All this time I have been loyal to the house of \zwd your father Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, to his brothers, and to his friends. I have not delivered you into the hand of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, but now you accuse me of wrongdoing with \zwd this woman|Q2899077\zwd*! 
\v 9 May God punish \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, and ever so severely, if I do not do for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* what the LORD has sworn to him: 
\v 10 to transfer the kingdom from the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and to establish the throne of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* over Israel and Judah, from Dan to Beersheba.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And for fear of \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* did not dare to say another word to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* sent messengers on his behalf to say to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “To whom does the land belong? Make your covenant with me, and surely my hand will be with you to bring all Israel over to you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 “Good,” replied \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “I will make a covenant with you. But there is one thing I require of you: Do not appear before me unless you bring \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* when you come to see me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent messengers to say to \zwd Ish-bosheth son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q313238\zwd*, “Give me back \zwd my wife|Q1341397\zwd*, \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd*, whom I betrothed to myself for a hundred Philistine foreskins.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* sent and took \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd* from \zwd her husband Paltiel|Q7128770\zwd* \f + \fr 3:15 \ft Paltiel is a variant of Palti; see 1 Samuel 25:44.\f* son of \zwd Laish|id="Q106096033" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 16 \zwd Her husband|Q7128770\zwd* followed her, weeping all the way to Bahurim. Then \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* said to him, “Go back.” So he returned home. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Now \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* conferred with the elders of Israel and said, “In the past you sought \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* as your king. 
\v 18 Now take action, because the LORD has said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, ‘Through \zwd My servant David|Q41370\zwd* I will save My people Israel from the hands of the Philistines and of all their enemies.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* also spoke to the Benjamites. Then he went to Hebron to tell \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* all that seemed good to Israel and to the whole house of Benjamin. 
\v 20 When \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* and twenty of his men came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* at Hebron, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* held a feast for them. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Let me go at once, and I will gather all Israel to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, that they may make a covenant with you, and that you may rule over all that your heart desires.” 
\b
\m So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* dismissed \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, and he went in peace. 
\s1 Joab Murders Abner
\b
\m 
\v 22 Just then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s soldiers and \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* returned from a raid, bringing with them a great plunder. But \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* was not with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* in Hebron, because \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had sent him on his way in peace. 
\v 23 When \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and all his troops arrived, he was informed, “\zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd* came to see \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, who sent him on his way in peace.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* went to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and said, “What have you done? Look, \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* came to you. Why did you dismiss him? Now he is getting away! 
\v 25 Surely you realize that \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd* came to deceive you and to track your movements and all that you are doing.” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 As soon as \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* had left \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he sent messengers after \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*, who brought him back from the well of Sirah. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was unaware of it. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* returned to Hebron, \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* pulled him aside into the gateway, as if to speak to him privately, and there \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* stabbed him in the stomach. So \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* died on account of the blood of \zwd \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s brother Asahel|id="Q2915076" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Afterward, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* heard about this and said, “I and my kingdom are forever guiltless before the LORD concerning the blood of \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|Q1133337\zwd*. 
\v 29 May it whirl over the head of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and over the entire house of his father, and may the house of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* never be without one having a discharge or skin disease, or one who leans on a staff or falls by the sword or lacks food.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 (\zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and \zwd his brother Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* murdered \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* because he had killed \zwd their brother Asahel|id="Q2915076" t="2"\zwd* in the battle at Gibeon.) 
\s1 David Mourns for Abner
\b
\m 
\v 31 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* ordered \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and all the people with him, “Tear your clothes, put on sackcloth, and mourn before \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*.” And \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* himself walked behind the funeral bier. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 When they buried \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* in Hebron, \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* wept aloud at \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*’s tomb, and all the people wept. 
\v 33 And \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* sang this lament for \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 “Should \zwd Abner|Q1133337\zwd* die 
\q2 the death of a fool? 
\q1 
\v 34 Your hands were not bound, 
\q2 your feet were not fettered. 
\q1 As a man falls before the wicked, 
\q2 so also you fell.” 
\b
\m And all the people wept over him even more. 
\b
\m 
\v 35 Then all the people came and urged \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to eat something while it was still day, but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took an oath, saying, “May God punish me, and ever so severely, if I taste bread or anything else before the sun sets!” 
\b
\m 
\v 36 All the people took note and were pleased. In fact, everything \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* did pleased them. 
\v 37 So on that day all the troops and all Israel were convinced that \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* had no part in the murder of \zwd Abner son of \zwd Ner|id="Q132145387" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q1133337" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to his servants, “Do you not realize that \zwd a great prince|id="Q1133337" t="2"\zwd* has fallen today in Israel? 
\v 39 And I am weak this day, though anointed as king, and these men, the sons of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*, are too fierce for me. May the LORD repay the evildoer according to his evil!” 
\c 4
\s1 The Murder of Ish-bosheth
\m 
\v 1 Now when \zwd Ish-bosheth son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q313238\zwd* heard that \zwd Abner|id="Q1133337" t="2"\zwd* had died in Hebron, he lost courage, and all Israel was dismayed. 
\v 2 \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s son|Q313238\zwd* had \zwd two men|\zwd* who were leaders of raiding parties. One was named \zwd Baanah|Q132166836\zwd* and the other \zwd Rechab|\zwd*; they were sons of \zwd Rimmon the Beerothite|\zwd* of the tribe of Benjamin—Beeroth is considered part of Benjamin, 
\v 3 because the Beerothites fled to Gittaim and have lived there as foreigners to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And \zwd Jonathan son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* had \zwd a son|Q851686\zwd* who was lame in his feet. He was five years old when the report about \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* came from Jezreel. His nurse picked him up and fled, but as she was hurrying to escape, he fell and became lame. His name was \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zdate|Q313238:P570\*Now \zwd Rechab|\zwd* and \zwd Baanah|Q132166836\zwd*, the sons of \zwd Rimmon the Beerothite|\zwd*, set out and arrived at the house of \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* in the heat of the day, while \zwd the king|Q313238\zwd* was taking his midday nap. 
\v 6 They entered the interior of the house as if to get some wheat, and they stabbed him in the stomach. Then \zwd Rechab|\zwd* and \zwd his brother Baanah|Q132166836\zwd* slipped away. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 They had entered the house while \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* was lying on his bed, and having stabbed and killed him, they beheaded him, took his head, and traveled all night by way of the Arabah. 
\v 8 They brought the head of \zwd Ish-bosheth|Q313238\zwd* to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* at Hebron and said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Here is the head of \zwd Ish-bosheth son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q313238\zwd*, your enemy who sought your life. Today the LORD has granted vengeance to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* against \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and his offspring.” 
\s1 The Execution of Rechab and Baanah
\b
\m 
\v 9 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* answered \zwd Rechab|\zwd* and \zwd his brother Baanah|Q132166836\zwd*, the sons of \zwd Rimmon the Beerothite|\zwd*, “As surely as the LORD lives, who has redeemed my life from all distress, 
\v 10 when someone told me, ‘Look, \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* is dead,’ and thought he was a bearer of good news, I seized him and put him to death at Ziklag. That was his reward for his news! 
\v 11 How much more, when wicked men kill a righteous man in his own house and on his own bed, shall I not now require his blood from your hands and remove you from the earth!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* commanded his young men, and they killed \zwd Rechab|\zwd* and \zwd Baanah|Q132166836\zwd*. They cut off their hands and feet and hung their bodies by the pool in Hebron, but they took the head of \zwd Ish-bosheth|id="Q313238" t="2"\zwd* and buried it in \zwd Abner|id="Q1133337" t="2"\zwd*’s tomb in Hebron. 
\c 5
\s1 David Anointed King of All Israel
\r (1 Chronicles 11:1–3)
\m 
\v 1 \zdate|Q41370:P39:Q43459871:P580\*Then all the tribes of Israel came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* at Hebron and said, “Here we are, your own flesh and blood. 
\v 2 Even in times past, while \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* was king over us, you were the one who led Israel out and brought them back. And to you the LORD said, ‘You will shepherd My people Israel, and you will be ruler over them.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So all the elders of Israel came to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* at Hebron, where \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* made with them a covenant before the LORD. And they anointed him king over Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was thirty years old when he became king, and he reigned forty years. 
\v 5 In Hebron he reigned over Judah seven years and six months, and in Jerusalem he reigned thirty-three years over all Israel and Judah. 
\s1 David Conquers Jerusalem
\r (1 Chronicles 11:4–9)
\b
\m 
\v 6 Now \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and his men marched to Jerusalem against the Jebusites who inhabited the land. The Jebusites said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*: “You will never get in here. Even the blind and lame can repel you.” For they thought, “\zwd David|Q41370\zwd* cannot get in here.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Nevertheless, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* captured the fortress of Zion (that is, the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*). 
\v 8 On that day he said, “Whoever attacks the Jebusites must use the water shaft to reach the lame and blind who are despised by \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*.\f + \fr 5:8 \ft Or who are enemies of David\f*” That is why it is said, “The blind and the lame will never enter the palace.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took up residence in the fortress and called it the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. He built it up all the way around, from the supporting terraces \f + \fr 5:9 \ft Hebrew the Millo\f* inward. 
\v 10 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* became greater and greater, for the LORD God of Hosts was with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Now \zwd Hiram king of Tyre|Q467115\zwd* sent envoys to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, along with cedar logs, carpenters, and stonemasons, and they built a palace for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\s1 David’s Family Grows
\r (1 Chronicles 14:1–7)
\b
\m 
\v 12 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* realized that the LORD had established him as king over Israel and had exalted his kingdom for the sake of His people Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 After he had arrived from Hebron, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took more concubines and wives from Jerusalem, and more sons and daughters were born to him. 
\v 14 These are the names of the children born to him in Jerusalem: \zwd Shammua|Q133263412\zwd*,\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Shammua is a variant of Shimea; see 1 Chronicles 3:5.\f* \zwd Shobab|Q9352021\zwd*, \zwd Nathan|Q3437128\zwd*, \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*, 
\v 15 \zwd Ibhar|Q5984013\zwd*, \zwd Elishua|Q132170354\zwd*, \zwd Nepheg|Q133258642\zwd*, \zwd Japhia|Q128310030\zwd*, 
\v 16 \zwd Elishama|Q18246454\zwd*, \zwd Eliada|Q9252681\zwd*, and \zwd Eliphelet|Q132170591\zwd*. 
\s1 Two Victories over the Philistines
\r (1 Chronicles 14:8–17)
\b
\m 
\v 17 When the Philistines heard that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had been anointed king over Israel, they all went in search of him; but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* learned of this and went down to the stronghold. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now the Philistines had come and spread out in the Valley of Rephaim. 
\v 19 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD, “Should I go up against the Philistines? Will You deliver them into my hand?” 
\b
\m “Go up,” replied the LORD, “for I will surely deliver the Philistines into your hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went to Baal-perazim, where he defeated the Philistines and said, “Like a bursting flood, the LORD has burst out against my enemies before me.” So he called that place Baal-perazim.\f + \fr 5:20 \ft Baal-perazim means The Lord Bursts Out.\f* 
\v 21 There the Philistines abandoned their idols, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men carried them away. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Once again the Philistines came up and spread out in the Valley of Rephaim. 
\v 23 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* inquired of the LORD, who answered, “Do not march straight up, but circle around behind them and attack them in front of the balsam trees.\f + \fr 5:23 \ft Or aspen trees or poplar trees; also in verse 24\f* 
\v 24 As soon as you hear the sound of marching in the tops of the balsam trees, move quickly, because this will mean that the LORD has gone out before you to strike the camp of the Philistines.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* did as the LORD had commanded him, and he struck down the Philistines all the way from Gibeon \f + \fr 5:25 \ft LXX (see also 1 Chronicles 14:16); Hebrew Geba\f* to Gezer. 
\c 6
\s1 David Fetches the Ark
\r (1 Chronicles 13:1–7)
\m 
\v 1 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* again assembled the chosen men of Israel, thirty thousand in all. 
\v 2 And he and all his troops set out for Baale of Judah \f + \fr 6:2 \ft That is, Kiriath-jearim; see 1 Chronicles 13:6.\f* to bring up from there the ark of God, which is called by the Name—\f + \fr 6:2 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate do not include the Name—\f* the name of the LORD of Hosts, who is enthroned between the cherubim that are on it. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 They set the ark of God on a new cart and brought it from the house of \zwd Abinadab|\zwd*, which was on the hill. \zwd Uzzah|Q4331914\zwd* and \zwd Ahio|Q138713515\zwd*, the sons of \zwd Abinadab|\zwd*, were guiding the new cart, 
\v 4 bringing with it the ark of God.\f + \fr 6:4 \ft DSS and some LXX manuscripts; MT includes from the house of Abinadab, which was on the hill.\f* And \zwd Ahio|Q138713515\zwd* was walking in front of the ark. 
\s1 Uzzah Touches the Ark
\r (1 Chronicles 13:8–14)
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and all the house of Israel were celebrating before the LORD with all kinds of wood instruments,\f + \fr 6:5 \ft Literally before the LORD with all woods of cypress; LXX playing before the Lord on well-tuned instruments mightily, and with songs; some of the instruments in this verse are uncertain; see 1 Chronicles 13:8.\f* harps, stringed instruments, tambourines, sistrums, and cymbals. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When they came to the threshing floor of \zwd Nacon|id="Q97171041" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 6:6 \ft Nacon is a variant of Chidon; see 1 Chronicles 13:9.\f* \zwd Uzzah|Q4331914\zwd* reached out and took hold of the ark of God, because the oxen had stumbled. 
\v 7 And the anger of the LORD burned against \zwd Uzzah|Q4331914\zwd*, and God struck him down on the spot for his irreverence,\f + \fr 6:7 \ft MT; DSS for this\f* and he died there beside the ark of God. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* became angry because the LORD had burst forth against \zwd Uzzah|Q4331914\zwd*. So he named that place Perez-uzzah,\f + \fr 6:8 \ft Perez-uzzah means outbreak against Uzzah.\f* as it is called to this day. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 That day \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* feared the LORD and asked, “How can the ark of the LORD ever come to me?” 
\v 10 So he was unwilling to move the ark of the LORD to the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*; instead, he took it aside to the house of \zwd Obed-edom the Gittite|\zwd*. 
\v 11 Thus the ark of the LORD remained in the house of \zwd Obed-edom the Gittite|\zwd* for three months, and the LORD blessed him and all his household. 
\s1 The Ark Brought to Jerusalem
\r (1 Chronicles 15:1–28)
\b
\m 
\v 12 Now it was reported to \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd*, “The LORD has blessed the house of \zwd Obed-edom|\zwd* and all that belongs to him, because of the ark of God.” 
\b
\m So David went and had the ark of God brought up from the house of \zwd Obed-edom|\zwd* into the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* with rejoicing. 
\v 13 When those carrying the ark of the LORD had advanced six paces, he sacrificed an ox and a fattened calf. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, wearing a linen ephod, danced with all his might before the LORD, 
\v 15 while he and all the house of Israel brought up the ark of the LORD with shouting and the sounding of the ram’s horn. 
\s1 Michal’s Contempt for David
\r (1 Chronicles 15:29—16:3)
\b
\m 
\v 16 As the ark of the LORD was entering the City of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* looked down from a window and saw \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* leaping and dancing before the LORD, and she despised him in her heart. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So they brought the ark of the LORD and set it in its place inside the tent that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had pitched for it. Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* offered burnt offerings and peace offerings before the LORD. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had finished sacrificing the burnt offerings and peace offerings, he blessed the people in the name of the LORD of Hosts. 
\v 19 Then he distributed to every man and woman among the multitude of Israel a loaf of bread, a date cake,\f + \fr 6:19 \ft Or a portion of meat\f* and a raisin cake. And all the people departed, each to his own home. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* returned home to bless his own household, \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s daughter Michal|Q1341397\zwd* came out to meet him. “How \zwd the king of Israel|Q41370\zwd* has distinguished himself today!” she said. “He has uncovered himself today in the sight of the maidservants of his subjects, like a vulgar person would do.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Michal|Q1341397\zwd*, “I was dancing before the LORD, who chose me over \zwd your father|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and all his house when He appointed me ruler over the LORD’s people Israel. I will celebrate before the LORD, 
\v 22 and I will humiliate and humble myself even more than this. Yet I will be honored by the maidservants of whom you have spoken.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 And \zwd Michal the daughter of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q1341397\zwd* had no children to the day of her death. 
\c 7
\s1 God’s Covenant with David
\r (1 Chronicles 17:1–15)
\m 
\v 1 After \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* had settled into his palace and the LORD had given him rest from all his enemies around him, 
\v 2 he said to \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd*, “Here I am, living in a house of cedar, while the ark of God remains in a tent.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 And \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* replied to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Go and do all that is in your heart, for the LORD is with you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 But that night the word of the LORD came to \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd*, saying, 
\v 5 “Go and tell \zwd My servant David|Q41370\zwd* that this is what the LORD says: Are you the one to build for Me a house to dwell in? 
\v 6 For I have not dwelt in a house from the day I brought the Israelites up out of Egypt until this day, but I have moved about with a tent as My dwelling. 
\v 7 In all My journeys with all the Israelites, have I ever asked any of the leaders \f + \fr 7:7 \ft Or tribes\f* I appointed to shepherd My people Israel, ‘Why haven’t you built Me a house of cedar?’ 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Now then, you are to tell \zwd My servant David|Q41370\zwd* that this is what the LORD of Hosts says: I took you from the pasture, from following the flock, to be the ruler over My people Israel. 
\v 9 I have been with you wherever you have gone, and I have cut off all your enemies from before you. Now I will make for you a name like that of the greatest in the land. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 And I will provide a place for My people Israel and will plant them so that they may dwell in a place of their own and be disturbed no more. No longer will the sons of wickedness oppress them as they did at the beginning 
\v 11 and have done since the day I appointed judges over My people Israel. I will give you rest from all your enemies. 
\b
\m The LORD declares to you that He Himself will establish a house for you. 
\v 12 And when your days are fulfilled and you rest with your fathers, I will raise up \zwd your descendant|\zwd* after you, who will come from your own body, and I will establish his kingdom. 
\v 13 He will build a house for My Name, and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever. 
\v 14 I will be his Father, and he will be \zwd My son|\zwd*.\f + \fr 7:14 \ft Cited in Hebrews 1:5\f* When he does wrong, I will discipline him with the rod of men and with the blows of the sons of men. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 But My loving devotion will never be removed from him as I removed it from \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, whom I removed from before you. 
\v 16 Your house and kingdom will endure forever before Me,\f + \fr 7:16 \ft Some Hebrew manuscripts and LXX; most Hebrew manuscripts you\f* and your throne will be established forever.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* relayed to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* all the words of this entire revelation. 
\s1 David’s Prayer of Thanksgiving
\r (1 Chronicles 17:16–27)
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* went in, sat before the LORD, and said, “Who am I, O Lord GOD, and what is my house, that You have brought me this far? 
\v 19 And as if this was a small thing in Your eyes, O Lord GOD, You have also spoken about the future of the house of \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*. Is this Your custom with man, O Lord GOD?\f + \fr 7:19 \ft Or And this is Your instruction for mankind, O Lord GOD.\f* 
\v 20 What more can \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* say to You? For You know \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*, O Lord GOD. 
\v 21 For the sake of Your word and according to Your own heart, You have accomplished this great thing and revealed it to \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 How great You are, O Lord GOD! For there is none like You, and there is no God but You, according to everything we have heard with our own ears. 
\v 23 And who is like Your people Israel—the one nation on earth whom God went out to redeem as a people for Himself and to make a name for Himself? You performed great and awesome wonders by driving out nations and their gods from before Your people, whom You redeemed for Yourself from Egypt.\f + \fr 7:23 \ft See LXX and 1 Chronicles 17:21; Hebrew You performed for Yourself great and awesome wonders for Your land, before Your people, whom You have redeemed for Yourself from Egypt, from nations and their gods.\f* 
\v 24 For You have established Your people Israel as Your very own forever, and You, O LORD, have become their God. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 And now, O LORD God, confirm forever the word You have spoken concerning \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* and his house. Do as You have promised, 
\v 26 so that Your name will be magnified forever when it is said, ‘The LORD of Hosts is God over Israel.’ And the house of \zwd Your servant David|Q41370\zwd* will be established before You. 
\v 27 For You, O LORD of Hosts, the God of Israel, have revealed this to \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* when You said, ‘I will build a house for you.’ Therefore \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* has found the courage to offer this prayer to You. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 And now, O Lord GOD, You are God! Your words are true, and You have promised this goodness to \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 29 Now therefore, may it please You to bless the house of \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*, that it may continue forever before You. For You, O Lord GOD, have spoken, and with Your blessing the house of \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd* will be blessed forever.” 
\c 8
\s1 David’s Triumphs
\r (1 Chronicles 18:1–13; Psalms 60:1–12)
\m 
\v 1 Some time later, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* defeated the Philistines, subdued them, and took Metheg-ammah \f + \fr 8:1 \ft Metheg-ammah means bridle of the mother city.\f* from the hand of the Philistines. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* also defeated the Moabites, made them lie down on the ground, and measured them off with a cord. He measured off with two lengths those to be put to death, and with one length those to be spared. So the Moabites became subject to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and brought him tribute. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* also defeated \zwd Hadadezer son of \zwd Rehob|id="Q97172306" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, king of Zobah, who had marched out to restore his dominion along the Euphrates River.\f + \fr 8:3 \ft LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate (see also 1 Chronicles 18:3); Hebrew along the River, or an alternate reading along the Euphrates\f* 
\v 4 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* captured from him a thousand chariots, seven thousand charioteers,\f + \fr 8:4 \ft LXX (see also DSS and 1 Chronicles 18:4); MT captured from him seventeen hundred charioteers\f* and twenty thousand foot soldiers, and he hamstrung all the horses except a hundred he kept for the chariots. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 When the Arameans of Damascus came to help \zwd King Hadadezer of Zobah|\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* struck down twenty-two thousand of their men. 
\v 6 Then he placed garrisons in Aram of Damascus, and the Arameans became subject to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and brought him tribute. So the LORD made \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* victorious wherever he went. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took the gold shields that belonged to the officers of \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd* and brought them to Jerusalem. 
\v 8 And from Betah \f + \fr 8:8 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts Tebah; see 1 Chronicles 18:8.\f* and Berothai, cities of \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd*, \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* took a large amount of bronze. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 When \zwd King Toi \f + \fr 8:9 \ft Toi is a variant of Tou; also in verse 10; see 1 Chronicles 18:9.\f* of Hamath|Q7828518\zwd* heard that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had defeated the entire army of \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd*, 
\v 10 he sent \zwd his son Joram|\zwd* \f + \fr 8:10 \ft Joram is a variant of Hadoram; see 1 Chronicles 18:10.\f* to greet \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* and bless him for fighting and defeating \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd*, who had been at war with \zwd Toi|Q7828518\zwd*. \zwd Joram|\zwd* brought with him articles of silver and gold and bronze, 
\v 11 and \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* dedicated these to the LORD, along with the silver and gold he had dedicated from all the nations he had subdued—
\v 12 from Edom \f + \fr 8:12 \ft Some Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, and Syriac (see also verse 14 and 1 Chronicles 18:11); most Hebrew manuscripts Aram\f* and Moab, from the Ammonites and Philistines and Amalekites, and from the spoil of \zwd Hadadezer son of \zwd Rehob|id="Q97172306" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, king of Zobah. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* made a name for himself when he returned from striking down eighteen thousand Edomites \f + \fr 8:13 \ft A few Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, and Syriac (see also verse 14 and 1 Chronicles 18:12); most Hebrew manuscripts Arameans\f* in the Valley of Salt. 
\v 14 He placed garrisons throughout Edom, and all the Edomites were subject to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. So the LORD made \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* victorious wherever he went. 
\s1 David’s Officers
\r (1 Chronicles 18:14–17)
\b
\m 
\v 15 Thus \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* reigned over all Israel and administered justice and righteousness for all his people: 
\b
\li1 
\v 16 \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd* was over the army; 
\b
\li1 \zwd Jehoshaphat son of \zwd Ahilud|id="Q132156345" t="2"\zwd*|Q20502574\zwd* was the recorder; 
\b
\li1 
\v 17 \zwd Zadok son of \zwd Ahitub|id="Q109376137" t="2"\zwd*|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Ahimelech son of \zwd Abiathar|\zwd*|\zwd* were priests; 
\b
\li1 \zwd Seraiah|Q124385844\zwd* \f + \fr 8:17 \ft Seraiah is also called Sheva, Shisha, and Shavsha; see 2 Samuel 20:25, 1 Kings 4:3, and 1 Chronicles 18:16.\f* was the scribe; 
\b
\li1 
\v 18 \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* was over the Cherethites and Pelethites; 
\b
\li1 and \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s sons|\zwd* were priestly leaders.\f + \fr 8:18 \ft Literally priests, or possibly chief officials; see LXX, Targum Yonaton, and 1 Chronicles 18:17.\f* 
\c 9
\s1 David and Mephibosheth
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Is there anyone left from the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* to whom I can show kindness for the sake of \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 And there was a servant of the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* named \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd*. They summoned him to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, and the king inquired, “Are you \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “I am your servant,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Is there anyone left of the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* to whom I can show the kindness of God?” 
\b
\m \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* answered, “There is still \zwd \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*’s son|Q851686\zwd*, who is lame in both feet.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “Where is he?” replied \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m And \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* said, “Indeed, he is in Lo-debar at the house of \zwd Machir son of \zwd Ammiel|\zwd*|Q18222843\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* had him brought from the house of \zwd Machir son of \zwd Ammiel|\zwd*|Q18222843\zwd* in Lo-debar. 
\v 6 And when \zwd Mephibosheth \f + \fr 9:6 \ft Mephibosheth is also called Merib-baal; see 1 Chronicles 8:34.\f* son of \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*|Q851686\zwd*, the son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he fell facedown in reverence. 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, \zwd “Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*!” 
\b
\m “I am your servant,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 “Do not be afraid,” said \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “for surely I will show you kindness for the sake of \zwd your father Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*. I will restore to you all the land of \zwd your grandfather Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, and you will always eat at my table.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* bowed down and said, “What is your servant, that you should show regard for a dead dog like me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* summoned \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s servant Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* and said to him, “I have given to \zwd \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s grandson|Q851686\zwd* all that belonged to \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and to all his house. 
\v 10 You and your sons and servants are to work the ground for him and bring in the harvest, so that \zwd \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s grandson|Q851686\zwd* may have food to eat. But \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*, \zwd \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s grandson|Q851686\zwd*, is always to eat at my table.” 
\b
\m Now \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* had fifteen sons and twenty servants. 
\v 11 And \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Your servant will do all that \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* has commanded.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* ate at \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s table \f + \fr 9:11 \ft LXX; Hebrew my table\f* like one of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s own sons. 
\v 12 And \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* had a young son named \zwd Mica|\zwd*,\f + \fr 9:12 \ft Mica is a variant of Micah; see 1 Chronicles 8:34.\f* and all who dwelt in the house of \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* were servants of \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*. 
\v 13 So \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* lived in Jerusalem, because he always ate at \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s table, and he was lame in both feet. 
\c 10
\s1 David’s Messengers Disgraced
\r (1 Chronicles 19:1–9)
\m 
\v 1 Some time later, \zwd the king of the Ammonites|Q6959218\zwd* died and was succeeded by \zwd his son Hanun|Q3334052\zwd*. 
\v 2 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said, “I will show kindness to \zwd Hanun son of \zwd Nahash|id="Q6959218" t="2"\zwd*|Q3334052\zwd*, just as \zwd his father|id="Q6959218" t="2"\zwd* showed kindness to me.” 
\b
\m So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent some of his servants to console \zwd Hanun|Q3334052\zwd* concerning \zwd his father|id="Q6959218" t="2"\zwd*. But when they arrived in the land of the Ammonites, 
\v 3 the princes of the Ammonites said to \zwd Hanun their lord|Q3334052\zwd*, “Just because \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* has sent you comforters, do you really believe he is showing respect for \zwd your father|id="Q6959218" t="2"\zwd*? Has not \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* instead sent his servants to explore the city, spy it out, and overthrow it?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd Hanun|Q3334052\zwd* took \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s servants, shaved off half of each man’s beard, cut off their garments at the hips, and sent them away. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 When this was reported to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he sent messengers to meet the men, since they had been thoroughly humiliated. \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* told them, “Stay in Jericho until your beards have grown back, and then return.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When the Ammonites realized that they had become a stench to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, they hired twenty thousand Aramean foot soldiers from Beth-rehob and Zoba, as well as a thousand men from \zwd the king of Maacah|\zwd* and twelve thousand men from Tob. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 On hearing this, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and the entire army of mighty men. 
\v 8 The Ammonites marched out and arrayed themselves for battle at the entrance of the city gate, while the Arameans of Zobah and \zwd Rehob|id="Q97172306" t="2"\zwd* and the men of Tob and Maacah were by themselves in the open country. 
\s1 David Defeats Ammon and Aram
\r (1 Chronicles 19:10–19)
\b
\m 
\v 9 When \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* saw the battle lines before him and behind him, he selected some of the best men of Israel and arrayed them against the Arameans. 
\v 10 And he placed the rest of the troops under the command of \zwd his brother Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, who arrayed them against the Ammonites. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “If the Arameans are too strong for me,” said \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “then you will come to my rescue. And if the Ammonites are too strong for you, then I will come to your rescue. 
\v 12 Be strong and let us fight bravely for our people and for the cities of our God. May the LORD do what is good in His sight.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and his troops advanced to fight the Arameans, who fled before him. 
\v 14 When the Ammonites saw that the Arameans had fled, they too fled before \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, and they entered the city. So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* returned from fighting against the Ammonites and came to Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When the Arameans saw that they had been defeated by Israel, they regrouped. 
\v 16 \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd* sent messengers to bring more Arameans from beyond the Euphrates,\f + \fr 10:16 \ft Hebrew the River\f* and they came to Helam with Shobach \f + \fr 10:16 \ft Shobach is a variant of Shophach; see 1 Chronicles 19:16.\f* \zwd the commander of \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd*’s army|\zwd* leading them. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When this was reported to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, he gathered all Israel, crossed the Jordan, and went to Helam. Then the Arameans arrayed themselves against \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and fought against him. 
\v 18 But the Arameans fled before Israel, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* killed seven hundred charioteers and forty thousand foot soldiers.\f + \fr 10:18 \ft Some LXX manuscripts (see also 1 Chronicles 19:18); Hebrew horsemen\f* He also struck down \zwd Shobach the commander of their army|\zwd*, who died there. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 When all the kings who were subject to \zwd Hadadezer|\zwd* saw that they had been defeated by Israel, they made peace with Israel and became subject to them. So the Arameans were afraid to help the Ammonites anymore. 
\c 11
\s1 David and Bathsheba
\m 
\v 1 In the spring,\f + \fr 11:1 \ft Literally At the turn of the year\f* at the time when kings march out to war, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent out \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and his servants with the whole army of Israel. They destroyed the Ammonites and besieged Rabbah, but \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* remained in Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 One evening \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up from his bed and strolled around on the roof of the palace. And from the roof he saw \zwd a woman|Q272277\zwd* bathing—\zwd a very beautiful woman|Q272277\zwd*. 
\v 3 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent and inquired about \zwd the woman|Q272277\zwd*, and he was told, “This is \zwd Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd*, the daughter of \zwd Eliam|id="Q13408496" t="2"\zwd* \f + \fr 11:3 \ft Eliam is a variant of Ammiel; see 1 Chronicles 3:5.\f* and the wife of \zwd Uriah the Hittite|Q71267\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent messengers to get her, and when she came to him, he slept with her. (Now she had just purified herself from her uncleanness.) Then she returned home. 
\v 5 And \zwd the woman|Q272277\zwd* conceived and sent word to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, saying, “I am pregnant.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 At this, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent orders to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*: “Send me \zwd Uriah the Hittite|Q71267\zwd*.” So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* sent him to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 When \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* came to him, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked how \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and the troops were doing and how the war was going. 
\v 8 Then he said to \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd*, “Go down to your house and wash your feet.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* left the palace, and a gift from \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* followed him. 
\v 9 But \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* slept at the door of the palace with all \zwd his master|Q41370\zwd*’s servants; he did not go down to his house. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was told, “\zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* did not go home.” 
\b
\m “Haven’t you just arrived from a journey?” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd*. “Why didn’t you go home?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* answered, “The ark and Israel and Judah are dwelling in tents,\f + \fr 11:11 \ft Or staying at Sukkoth\f* and \zwd my master Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and his soldiers are camped in the open field. How can I go to my house to eat and drink and sleep with \zwd my wife|Q272277\zwd*? As surely as you live, and as your soul lives, I will not do such a thing!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “Stay here one more day,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd*, “and tomorrow I will send you back.” So \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* stayed in Jerusalem that day and the next. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* invited \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* to eat and drink with him, and he got \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* drunk. And in the evening \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* went out to lie down on his cot with \zwd his master|Q41370\zwd*’s servants, but he did not go home. 
\s1 David Arranges Uriah’s Death
\b
\m 
\v 14 The next morning \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* wrote a letter to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and sent it with \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd*. 
\v 15 In the letter he wrote: “Put \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* at the front of the fiercest battle; then withdraw from him, so that he may be struck down and killed.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So as \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* besieged the city, he assigned \zwd Uriah|Q71267\zwd* to a place where he knew the strongest enemy soldiers were. 
\v 17 And when the men of the city came out and fought against \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, some of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s servants fell, and \zwd Uriah the Hittite|Q71267\zwd* also died. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* sent to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* a full account of the battle 
\v 19 and instructed the messenger, “When you have finished giving \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* a full account of the battle, 
\v 20 if \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s anger flares, he may ask you, ‘Why did you get so close to the city to fight? Did you not realize they would shoot from atop the wall? 
\v 21 Who struck \zwd Abimelech son of \zwd Jerubbesheth|\zwd*|\zwd* \f + \fr 11:21 \ft Jerubbesheth is also known as Jerubbaal, that is, Gideon.\f*? Was it not a woman who dropped an upper millstone on him from the wall, so that he died in Thebez? Why did you get so close to the wall?’ 
\b
\m If he asks you this, then you are to say, ‘\zwd Your servant Uriah the Hittite|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd* is dead as well.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So the messenger set out and reported to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* all that \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* had sent him to say. 
\v 23 The messenger said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “The men overpowered us and came out against us in the field, but we drove them back to the entrance of the gate. 
\v 24 Then the archers shot at your servants from the wall, and some of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s servants were killed. And \zwd your servant Uriah the Hittite|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd* is dead as well.” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* told the messenger, “Say this to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*: ‘Do not let this matter upset you, for the sword devours one as well as another. Strengthen your attack against the city and demolish it.’ Encourage him with these words.” 
\s1 David Marries Bathsheba
\b
\m 
\v 26 When \zwd \zwd Uriah|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd*’s wife|Q272277\zwd* heard that \zwd her husband|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd* was dead, she mourned for him. 
\v 27 And when the time of mourning was over, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had her brought to his house, and she became \zwd his wife|Q272277\zwd* and bore him \zwd a son|\zwd*. 
\b
\m But the thing that \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had done was evil in the sight of the LORD. 
\c 12
\s1 Nathan Rebukes David
\r (Psalms 51:1–19)
\m 
\v 1 Then the LORD sent \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, and when he arrived, he said, “There were two men in a certain city, one rich and the other poor. 
\v 2 The rich man had a great number of sheep and cattle, 
\v 3 but the poor man had nothing except one small ewe lamb that he had bought. He raised it, and it grew up with him and his children. It shared his food and drank from his cup; it slept in his arms \f + \fr 12:3 \ft Hebrew in his bosom; also in verse 8\f* and was like a daughter to him. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now a traveler came to the rich man, who refrained from taking one of his own sheep or cattle to prepare for the traveler who had come to him. Instead, he took the poor man’s lamb and prepared it for his guest.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* burned with anger against the man and said to \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd*: “As surely as the LORD lives, the man who did this deserves to die! 
\v 6 Because he has done this thing and has shown no pity, he must pay for the lamb four times over.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “You are that man! This is what the LORD, the God of Israel, says: ‘I anointed you king over Israel, and I delivered you from the hand of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 8 I gave \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s house to you and \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s wives into your arms. I gave you the house of Israel and Judah, and if that was not enough, I would have given you even more. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Why then have you despised the command of the LORD by doing evil in His sight? You put \zwd Uriah the Hittite|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd* to the sword and took \zwd his wife|Q272277\zwd* as your own. You have slain him with the sword of the Ammonites. 
\v 10 Now, therefore, the sword will never depart from your house, because you have despised Me and have taken \zwd the wife of \zwd Uriah the Hittite|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd*|Q272277\zwd* to be your own.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 11 This is what the LORD says: ‘I will raise up adversity against you from your own house. Before your very eyes I will take your wives and give them to another, and he will lie with them in broad daylight. 
\v 12 You have acted in secret, but I will do this thing in broad daylight before all Israel.’” 
\s1 David’s Loss and Repentance
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd*, “I have sinned against the LORD.” 
\b
\m “The LORD has taken away your sin,” \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* replied. “You will not die. 
\v 14 Nevertheless, because by this deed you have shown utter contempt for the word of the LORD,\f + \fr 12:14 \ft DSS; MT you have brought utter contempt from the enemies of the LORD\f* \zwd the son born to you|\zwd* will surely die.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 After \zwd Nathan|Q319116\zwd* had gone home, the LORD struck \zwd the child that \zwd Uriah|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd*’s wife had borne to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*|\zwd*, and he became ill. 
\v 16 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* pleaded with God for the boy. He fasted and went into his house and spent the night lying in sackcloth \f + \fr 12:16 \ft DSS and LXX; MT does not include in sackcloth.\f* on the ground. 
\v 17 The elders of his household stood beside him to help him up from the ground, but he was unwilling and would not eat anything with them. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 On the seventh day \zwd the child|\zwd* died. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s servants were afraid to tell him that \zwd the child|\zwd* was dead, for they said, “Look, while \zwd the child|\zwd* was alive, we spoke to him, and he would not listen to us. So how can we tell him \zwd the child|\zwd* is dead? He may even harm himself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 19 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* saw that his servants were whispering to one another, he perceived that \zwd the child|\zwd* was dead. So he asked his servants, “Is \zwd the child|\zwd* dead?” 
\b
\m “He is dead,” they replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up from the ground, washed and anointed himself, changed his clothes, and went into the house of the LORD and worshiped. Then he went to his own house, and at his request they set food before him, and he ate. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 “What is this you have done?” his servants asked. “While \zwd the child|\zwd* was alive, you fasted and wept, but when he died, you got up and ate.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* answered, “While \zwd the child|\zwd* was alive, I fasted and wept, for I said, ‘Who knows? The LORD may be gracious to me and let him live.’ 
\v 23 But now that he is dead, why should I fast? Can I bring him back again? I will go to him, but he will not return to me.” 
\s1 Solomon’s Birth
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* comforted \zwd his wife Bathsheba|Q272277\zwd*, and he went to her and lay with her. So she gave birth to \zwd a son|Q37085\zwd*, and they \f + \fr 12:24 \ft Or she or he\f* named him \zwd Solomon|Q37085\zwd*. 
\b
\m Now the LORD loved \zwd the child|Q37085\zwd* 
\v 25 and sent word through \zwd Nathan the prophet|Q319116\zwd* to name him Jedidiah because the LORD loved him.\f + \fr 12:25 \ft Literally to name him Jedidiah because of the LORD; Jedidiah means beloved of the LORD\f* 
\s1 The Capture of Rabbah
\r (1 Chronicles 20:1–3)
\b
\m 
\v 26 Meanwhile, \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* fought against Rabbah of the Ammonites and captured the royal fortress. 
\v 27 Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* sent messengers to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to say, “I have fought against Rabbah and have captured the water supply of the city.\f + \fr 12:27 \ft Or and have captured the city of waters\f* 
\v 28 Now, therefore, assemble the rest of the troops, lay siege to the city, and capture it. Otherwise I will capture the city, and it will be named after me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* assembled all the troops and went to Rabbah; and he fought against it and captured it. 
\v 30 Then he took the crown from the head of \zwd their king|\zwd*.\f + \fr 12:30 \ft Or from the head of Milcom. Milcom, also called Molech, was god of the Ammonites; see Leviticus 18:21 and 1 Kings 11:7.\f* It weighed a talent of gold \f + \fr 12:30 \ft A talent is approximately 75.4 pounds or 34.2 kilograms of gold.\f* and was set with precious stones, and it was placed on \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s head. And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* took a great amount of plunder from the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* brought out the people who were there and put them to work with saws,\f + \fr 12:31 \ft Literally put them under saws\f* iron picks, and axes, and he made them work at the brick kilns.\f + \fr 12:31 \ft Literally and he made them pass through the brick kilns.\f* He did the same to all the Ammonite cities. Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and all his troops returned to Jerusalem. 
\c 13
\s1 Amnon and Tamar
\m 
\v 1 After some time, \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s son Amnon|Q361636\zwd* fell in love with \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*, the beautiful sister of \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s son Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\v 2 \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* was sick with frustration over \zwd his sister Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*, for she was a virgin, and it seemed implausible for him to do anything to her. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Now \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* had a friend named \zwd Jonadab|Q6271831\zwd*, the son of \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s brother Shimeah|id="Q16130530" t="2"\zwd*.\f + \fr 13:3 \ft Shimeah is a variant of Shammah, Shimea, and Shimei; see 1 Samuel 16:9, 2 Samuel 21:21, and 1 Chronicles 2:13.\f* \zwd Jonadab|Q6271831\zwd* was a very shrewd man, 
\v 4 and he asked \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd*, “Why are you, the son of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, so depressed morning after morning? Won’t you tell me?” 
\b
\m \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* replied, “I am in love with \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*, \zwd my brother Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s sister.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Jonadab|Q6271831\zwd* told him, “Lie down on your bed and pretend you are ill. When \zwd your father|Q41370\zwd* comes to see you, say to him, ‘Please let \zwd my sister Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* come and give me something to eat. Let her prepare it in my sight so I may watch her and eat it from her hand.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* lay down and feigned illness. When \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* came to see him, \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* said, “Please let \zwd my sister Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* come and make a couple of cakes in my sight, so that I may eat from her hand.” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sent word to \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* at the palace: “Please go to the house of \zwd Amnon your brother|Q361636\zwd* and prepare a meal for him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 So \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* went to the house of \zwd her brother Amnon|Q361636\zwd*, who was lying down. She took some dough, kneaded it, made cakes in his sight, and baked them. 
\v 9 Then she brought the pan and set it down before him, but he refused to eat. “Send everyone away!” said \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd*. And everyone went out. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* said to \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*, “Bring the food into the bedroom, so that I may eat it from your hand.” 
\b
\m \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* took the cakes she had made and went to \zwd her brother Amnon|Q361636\zwd*’s bedroom. 
\v 11 And when she had brought them to him to eat, he took hold of her and said, “Come lie with me, \zwd my sister|Q2917173\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “No, \zwd my brother|Q361636\zwd*!” she cried. “Do not violate me, for such a thing should never be done in Israel. Do not do this disgraceful thing! 
\v 13 Where could I ever take my shame? And you would be like one of the fools in Israel! Please speak to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, for he will not withhold me from you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* refused to listen to her, and being stronger, he violated her and lay with her. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* hated \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* with such intensity that his hatred was greater than the love he previously had. “Get up!” he said to her. “Be gone!” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 “No,” she replied, “sending me away is worse than this great wrong you have already done to me!” 
\b
\m But he refused to listen to her. 
\v 17 Instead, he called to his attendant and said, “Throw \zwd this woman|Q2917173\zwd* out and bolt the door behind her!” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 So \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd*’s attendant threw her out and bolted the door behind her. Now \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* was wearing a robe of many colors,\f + \fr 13:18 \ft Or a robe with long sleeves; see also Genesis 37:3.\f* because this is what \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s virgin daughters wore. 
\v 19 And \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* put ashes on her head and tore her robe. And putting her hand on her head, she went away crying aloud. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 \zwd Her brother Absalom|Q205372\zwd* said to her, “Has \zwd your brother Amnon|Q361636\zwd* been with you? Be quiet for now, \zwd my sister|Q2917173\zwd*. He is your brother. Do not take this thing to heart.” 
\b
\m So \zwd Tamar|Q2917173\zwd* lived as a desolate woman in the house of \zwd her brother Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 When \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* heard all this, he was furious.\f + \fr 13:21 \ft DSS and LXX include But he would not punish \zwd his son Amnon|Q361636\zwd*, because he loved him, since he was his firstborn.\f* 
\v 22 And \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* never said a word to \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd*, either good or bad, because he hated \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* for violating \zwd his sister Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*. 
\s1 Absalom’s Revenge on Amnon
\b
\m 
\v 23 Two years later, when \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s sheepshearers were at Baal-hazor near Ephraim, he invited all the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 24 And he went to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and said, “\zwd Your servant|Q205372\zwd* has just hired shearers. Will \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and his servants please come with me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 25 “No, \zwd my son|Q205372\zwd*,” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* replied, “we should not all go, or we would be a burden to you.” Although \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* urged him, he was not willing to go, but gave him his blessing. 
\b
\m 
\v 26 “If not,” said \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, “please let \zwd my brother Amnon|Q361636\zwd* go with us.” 
\b
\m “Why should he go with you?” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* asked. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 But \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* urged him, so \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* sent \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* and the rest of his sons.\f + \fr 13:27 \ft LXX and Vulgate include And Absalom prepared a feast fit for a king.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Now \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had ordered his young men, “Watch \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* until his heart is merry with wine, and when I order you to strike \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* down, you are to kill him. Do not be afraid. Have I not commanded you? Be courageous and valiant!” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s young men did to \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* just as \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had ordered. Then all the other sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* got up, and each one fled on his mule. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 While they were on the way, a report reached \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*: “\zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* has struck down all the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*; not one of them is left!” 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* stood up, tore his clothes, and lay down on the ground. And all his servants stood by with their clothes torn. 
\b
\m 
\v 32 But \zwd Jonadab|Q6271831\zwd*, the son of \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s brother Shimeah|id="Q16130530" t="2"\zwd*, spoke up: “\zwd My lord|Q41370\zwd* must not think they have killed all the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, for only \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* is dead. In fact, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* has planned this since the day \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* violated \zwd his sister Tamar|Q2917173\zwd*. 
\v 33 So now, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, do not take to heart the report that all the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* are dead. Only \zwd Amnon|Q361636\zwd* is dead.” 
\s1 Absalom Flees to Geshur
\b
\m 
\v 34 Meanwhile, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had fled. When the young man standing watch looked up, he saw many people coming down the road west of him,\f + \fr 13:34 \ft Or behind him\f* along the side of the hill. And the watchman went and reported to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “I see men coming from the direction of Horonaim, along the side of the hill.”\f + \fr 13:34 \ft LXX; Hebrew does not include And the watchman....\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 35 So \zwd Jonadab|Q6271831\zwd* said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Look, the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* have arrived! It is just as your servant said.” 
\b
\m 
\v 36 And as he finished speaking, the sons of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* came in, wailing loudly. Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and all his servants also wept very bitterly. 
\b
\m 
\v 37 Now \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* fled and went to \zwd Talmai son of \zwd Ammihud|\zwd*|Q33026733\zwd*, the king of Geshur. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* mourned for his son every day. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 After \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had fled and gone to Geshur, he stayed there three years. 
\v 39 And \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* \f + \fr 13:39 \ft MT; DSS and LXX And the spirit of the king \f* longed to go to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*,\f + \fr 13:39 \ft Or ceased to go out after Absalom\f* for he had been consoled over \zwd Amnon|id="Q361636" t="2"\zwd*’s death. 
\c 14
\s1 Absalom’s Return to Jerusalem
\m 
\v 1 Now \zwd Joab son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1141064\zwd* perceived that \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s heart longed for \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\v 2 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* sent to Tekoa to bring a wise woman from there. He told her, “Please pretend to be a mourner; put on clothes for mourning and do not anoint yourself with oil. Act like a woman who has mourned for the dead a long time. 
\v 3 Then go to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and speak these words to him.” And \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* put the words in her mouth. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 When the woman from Tekoa went to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, she fell facedown in homage and said, “Help me, O king!” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 “What troubles you?” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* asked her. 
\b
\m “Indeed,” she said, “I am a widow, for my husband is dead. 
\v 6 And your maidservant had two sons who were fighting in the field with no one to separate them, and one struck the other and killed him. 
\v 7 Now the whole clan has risen up against your maidservant and said, ‘Hand over the one who struck down his brother, that we may put him to death for the life of the brother whom he killed. Then we will cut off the heir as well!’ So they would extinguish my one remaining ember by not preserving my husband’s name or posterity on the earth.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 “Go home,” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to the woman, “and I will give orders on your behalf.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 But the woman of Tekoa said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “\zwd My lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, may any blame be on me and on my father’s house, and may \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and his throne be guiltless.” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 “If anyone speaks to you,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “bring him to me, and he will not trouble you again!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “Please,” she replied, “may \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* invoke the LORD your God to prevent the avenger of blood from increasing the devastation, so that my son may not be destroyed!” 
\b
\m “As surely as the LORD lives,” he vowed, “not a hair of your son’s head will fall to the ground.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then the woman said, “Please, may your servant speak a word to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “Speak,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 The woman asked, “Why have you devised a thing like this against the people of God? When \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* says this, does he not convict himself, since he has not brought back \zwd his own banished son|Q205372\zwd*? 
\v 14 For we will surely die and be like water poured out on the ground, which cannot be recovered. Yet God does not take away a life, but He devises ways that the banished one may not be cast out from Him. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 Now therefore, I have come to present this matter to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* because the people have made me afraid. Your servant thought, ‘I will speak to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. Perhaps he will grant the request of his maidservant. 
\v 16 For \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* will hear and deliver his maidservant from the hand of the man who would cut off both me and my son from God’s inheritance.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 17 And now your servant says, ‘May the word of \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* bring me rest, for \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* is able to discern good and evil, just like the angel \f + \fr 14:17 \ft Or Angel; also in verse 20\f* of God. May the LORD your God be with you.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to the woman, “I am going to ask you something; do not conceal it from me!” 
\b
\m “Let \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* speak,” she replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Is the hand of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* behind all this?” 
\b
\m The woman answered, “As surely as you live, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, no one can turn to the right or to the left from anything that \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* says. Yes, \zwd your servant Joab|Q1141064\zwd* is the one who gave me orders; he told your maidservant exactly what to say. 
\v 20 \zwd Joab your servant|Q1141064\zwd* has done this to bring about this change of affairs, but my lord has wisdom like the wisdom of the angel of God, to know everything that happens in the land.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “I hereby grant this request. Go, bring back \zwd the young man Absalom|Q205372\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* fell facedown in homage and blessed \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. “Today,” said \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “your servant knows that he has found favor in your eyes, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, because \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* has granted his request.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* got up, went to Geshur, and brought \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* to Jerusalem. 
\v 24 But \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* added, “He may return to his house, but he must not see my face.” So \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* returned to his own house, but he did not see the face of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Now there was not a man in all Israel as handsome and highly praised as \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. From the sole of his foot to the top of his head, he did not have a single flaw. 
\v 26 And when he cut the hair of his head—he shaved it every year because his hair got so heavy—he would weigh it out to be two hundred shekels,\f + \fr 14:26 \ft 200 shekels is approximately 5 pounds or 2.3 kilograms of hair.\f* according to the royal standard. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zwd Three sons|\zwd* were born to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, and a daughter named \zwd Tamar|Q30148909\zwd*, who was a beautiful woman. 
\s1 Absalom Reconciled to David
\b
\m 
\v 28 Now \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* lived in Jerusalem two years without seeing the face of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 29 Then he sent for \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* to send him to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, but \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* refused to come to him. 
\b
\m So \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* sent a second time, but \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* still would not come. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* said to his servants, “Look, \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s field is next to mine, and he has barley there. Go and set it on fire!” 
\b
\m And \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s servants set the field on fire.\f + \fr 14:30 \ft LXX includes So the servants of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* came to him with their clothes torn and said to him, “The servants of \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* have set your field on fire.”\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* came to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s house and demanded, “Why did your servants set my field on fire?” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 “Look,” said \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, “I sent for you and said, ‘Come here. I want to send you to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* to ask: Why have I come back from Geshur? It would be better for me if I were still there.’ So now, let me see \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s face, and if there is iniquity in me, let him kill me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* went and told \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* summoned \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, who came to him and bowed facedown before him. Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* kissed \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\c 15
\s1 Absalom’s Conspiracy
\m 
\v 1 Some time later, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* provided for himself a chariot with horses and fifty men to run ahead of him. 
\v 2 He would get up early and stand beside the road leading to the city gate. 
\b
\m Whenever anyone had a grievance to bring before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* for a decision, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* would call out and ask, “What city are you from?” And if he replied, “Your servant is from one of the tribes of Israel,” 
\v 3 \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* would say, “Look, your claims are good and right, but \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* has no deputy to hear you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And he would add, “If only someone would appoint me judge in the land, then everyone with a grievance or dispute could come to me, and I would give him justice.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Also, when anyone approached to bow down to him, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* would reach out his hand, take hold of him, and kiss him. 
\v 6 \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* did this to all the Israelites who came to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* for justice. In this way he stole the hearts of the men of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 After four \f + \fr 15:7 \ft Syriac and some LXX manuscripts; Hebrew forty\f* years had passed, \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Please let me go to Hebron to fulfill a vow I have made to the LORD. 
\v 8 For your servant made a vow while dwelling in Geshur of Aram, saying: ‘If indeed the LORD brings me back to Jerusalem, I will worship the LORD in Hebron.’\f + \fr 15:8 \ft Some LXX manuscripts; Hebrew does not include in Hebron.\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 “Go in peace,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. So \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* got up and went to Hebron. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* sent spies throughout the tribes of Israel with this message: “When you hear the sound of the horn, you are to say, ‘\zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* reigns in Hebron!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Two hundred men from Jerusalem accompanied \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. They had been invited as guests and they went along innocently, for they knew nothing about the matter. 
\v 12 While \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* was offering the sacrifices, he sent for \zwd Ahithophel the Gilonite|Q400294\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s counselor, to come from his hometown of Giloh. 
\b
\m So the conspiracy gained strength, and \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s following kept increasing. 
\s1 David Flees Jerusalem
\r (Psalms 3:1–8)
\b
\m 
\v 13 Then a messenger came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and reported, “The hearts of the men of Israel are with \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to all the servants with him in Jerusalem, “Arise and let us flee, or we will not escape from \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*! We must leave quickly, or he will soon overtake us, heap disaster on us, and put the city to the sword.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd*’s servants replied, “Whatever \zwd our lord the king|Q41370\zwd* decides, we are your servants.” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* set out, and his entire household followed him. But he left behind ten concubines to take care of the palace. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* set out with all the people following him. He stopped at the last house, 
\v 18 and all his servants marched past him—all the Cherethites and Pelethites, and six hundred Gittites who had followed him from Gath. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Ittai the Gittite|Q11714286\zwd*, “Why should you also go with us? Go back and stay with \zwd the new king|Q205372\zwd*, since you are both a foreigner and an exile from your homeland. 
\v 20 In fact, you arrived only yesterday; should I make you wander around with us today while I do not know where I am going? Go back and take your brothers with you. May the LORD show you loving devotion and faithfulness.\f + \fr 15:20 \ft LXX; Hebrew May loving devotion and faithfulness be with you.\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 But \zwd Ittai|Q11714286\zwd* answered \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “As surely as the LORD lives, and as \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* lives, wherever \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* may be, whether it means life or death, there will your servant be!” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 “March on then,” said \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to \zwd Ittai|Q11714286\zwd*. So \zwd Ittai the Gittite|Q11714286\zwd* marched past with all his men and all the little ones who were with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Everyone in the countryside was weeping loudly as all the people passed by. And as \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* crossed the Kidron Valley, all the people also passed toward the way of the wilderness. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* was also there, and all the Levites with him were carrying the ark of the covenant of God. And they set down the ark of God, and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* offered sacrifices \f + \fr 15:24 \ft Or Abiathar went up\f* until all the people had passed out of the city. 
\b
\m 
\v 25 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd*, “Return the ark of God to the city. If I find favor in the eyes of the LORD, He will bring me back and let me see both it and His dwelling place again. 
\v 26 But if He should say, ‘I do not delight in you,’ then here I am; let Him do to me whatever seems good to Him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 27 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* also said to \zwd Zadok the priest|Q140100\zwd*, “Are you not a seer?\f + \fr 15:27 \ft Hebrew; LXX Behold,\f* Return to the city in peace—you with \zwd your son Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd*, and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* with \zwd his son Jonathan|Q20502598\zwd*.\f + \fr 15:27 \ft Literally your son Ahimaaz and Jonathan son of Abiathar, your two sons with you\f* 
\v 28 See, I will wait at the fords of the wilderness until word comes from you to inform me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 So \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* returned the ark of God to Jerusalem and stayed there. 
\s1 David Weeps at the Mount of Olives
\r (Psalms 63:1–11)
\b
\m 
\v 30 But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* continued up the Mount of Olives, weeping as he went up. His head was covered, and he was walking barefoot. And all the people with him covered their heads and went up, weeping as they went. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Now someone told \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*: “\zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* is among the conspirators with \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*.” 
\b
\m So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* pleaded, “O LORD, please turn the counsel of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* into foolishness!” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* came to the summit, where he used to worship God, \zwd Hushai the Archite|Q5949258\zwd* was there to meet him with his robe torn and dust on his head. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to him, “If you go on with me, you will be a burden to me. 
\v 34 But you can thwart the counsel of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* for me if you return to the city and say to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*: ‘I will be your servant, \zwd my king|Q205372\zwd*; in the past I was \zwd your father|Q41370\zwd*’s servant, but now I will be your servant.’ 
\b
\m 
\v 35 Will not \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* the priests be there with you? Report to them everything you hear from \zwd the king|Q205372\zwd*’s palace. 
\v 36 Indeed, \zwd their two sons|Q2904547,Q20502598\zwd*, \zwd Ahimaaz son of \zwd Zadok|id="Q140100" t="2"\zwd*|Q2904547\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan son of \zwd Abiathar|id="Q321804" t="2"\zwd*|Q20502598\zwd*, are there with them. Send them to me with everything you hear.” 
\b
\m 
\v 37 So \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s friend Hushai|Q5949258\zwd* arrived in Jerusalem just as \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* was entering the city. 
\c 16
\s1 David and Ziba
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had gone a little beyond the summit, \zwd Ziba the servant of \zwd Mephibosheth|id="Q851686" t="2"\zwd*|Q8071402\zwd* was there to meet him. He had a pair of saddled donkeys loaded with two hundred loaves of bread, a hundred clusters of raisins, a hundred summer fruits, and a skin of wine. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 “Why do you have these?” asked \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* replied, “The donkeys are for \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s household to ride, the bread and summer fruit are for the young men to eat, and the wine is to refresh those who become exhausted in the wilderness.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 “Where is \zwd \zwd your master|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s grandson|id="Q851686" t="2"\zwd*?” asked \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m And \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* answered, “Indeed, he is staying in Jerusalem, for he has said, ‘Today, the house of Israel will restore to me the kingdom of \zwd my grandfather|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd*, “All that belongs to \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* is now yours!” 
\b
\m “I humbly bow before you,” said \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd*. “May I find favor in your eyes, \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*!” 
\s1 Shimei Curses David
\b
\m 
\v 5 As \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* approached Bahurim, a man from the family of the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* was just coming out. His name was \zwd Shimei son of \zwd Gera|\zwd*|Q6978950\zwd*, and as he approached, he kept yelling out curses. 
\v 6 He threw stones at \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and at all the servants of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, though the troops and all the mighty men were on \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s right and left. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 And as he yelled curses, \zwd Shimei|Q6978950\zwd* said, “Get out, get out, \zwd you worthless man of bloodshed|Q41370\zwd*! 
\v 8 The LORD has paid you back for all the blood of the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, in whose place you have reigned, and the LORD has delivered the kingdom into the hand of \zwd your son Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. See, you have come to ruin because you are a man of bloodshed!” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then \zwd Abishai son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1154317\zwd* said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Why should \zwd this dead dog|Q6978950\zwd* curse \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*? Let me go over and cut off his head!” 
\b
\m 
\v 10 But \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* replied, “What have I to do with you, O sons of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*? If he curses me because the LORD told him, ‘Curse \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*,’ who can ask, ‘Why did you do this?’” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* and to all his servants, “Behold, \zwd my own son|Q205372\zwd*, my own flesh and blood, seeks my life. How much more, then, this Benjamite! Leave him alone and let him curse me, for the LORD has told him so. 
\v 12 Perhaps the LORD will see my affliction and repay me with good for the cursing I receive today.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his men proceeded along the road as \zwd Shimei|Q6978950\zwd* went along the ridge of the hill opposite him. As \zwd Shimei|Q6978950\zwd* went, he yelled curses, threw stones, and flung dust at \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 14 Finally, \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and all the people with him arrived,\f + \fr 16:14 \ft Some LXX manuscripts arrived at the Jordan\f* exhausted. And there he refreshed himself. 
\s1 The Counsel of Ahithophel and Hushai
\b
\m 
\v 15 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* and all the men of Israel came to Jerusalem, and \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* was with him. 
\v 16 And \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s friend Hushai the Archite|Q5949258\zwd* went to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* and said to him, “Long live \zwd the king|Q205372\zwd*! Long live \zwd the king|Q205372\zwd*!” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 “Is this the loyalty you show \zwd your friend|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*?” \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* replied. “Why did you not go with \zwd your friend|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 “Not at all,” \zwd Hushai|Q5949258\zwd* answered. “For the one chosen by the LORD, by this  people, and by all the men of Israel—his I will be, and with him I will remain. 
\v 19 Furthermore, whom should I serve if not \zwd his son|Q205372\zwd*? As I served in \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s presence, so also I will serve in yours.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* said to \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd*, “Give me counsel. What should we do?” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* replied, “Sleep with \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s concubines, whom he has left to take care of the palace. When all Israel hears that you have become a stench to \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, then the hands of all who are with you will be strengthened.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So they pitched a tent for \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* on the roof, and he slept with \zwd his father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*’s concubines in the sight of all Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Now in those days the advice of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* was like the consultation of the word of God. Such was the regard that both \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had for \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd*’s advice. 
\c 17
\s1 Hushai Counters Ahithophel’s Advice
\m 
\v 1 Furthermore, \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* said to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, “Let me choose twelve thousand men and set out tonight in pursuit of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 2 I will attack him while he is weak and weary; I will throw him into a panic, and all the people with him will flee; I will strike down only \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* 
\v 3 and bring all the people back to you as a bride returning to her husband. You seek the life of only one man;\f + \fr 17:3 \ft LXX; see also DSS; MT and bring all the people back to you. When everyone returns except the man you seek,\f* then all the people will be at peace.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 This proposal seemed good to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* and all the elders of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* said, “Summon \zwd Hushai the Archite|Q5949258\zwd* as well, and let us hear what he too has to say.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd Hushai|Q5949258\zwd* came to \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, who told him, “\zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* has spoken this proposal. Should we carry it out? If not, what do you say?” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 \zwd Hushai|Q5949258\zwd* replied, “This time the advice of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* is not sound.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 He continued, “You know \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* and his men. They are mighty men, and as fierce as a wild bear robbed of her cubs. Moreover, \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* is a man of war who will not spend the night with the troops. 
\v 9 Surely by now he is hiding in a cave or some other location. If some of your troops fall first,\f + \fr 17:9 \ft Or If he should attack your troops first\f* whoever hears of it will say, ‘There has been a slaughter among the troops who follow \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*.’ 
\v 10 Then even the most valiant soldier with the heart of a lion will melt with fear, because all Israel knows that \zwd your father|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd* is a mighty man who has valiant men with him. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Instead, I advise that all Israel from Dan to Beersheba—a multitude like the sand on the seashore—be gathered to you, and that you yourself lead them into battle. 
\v 12 Then we will attack \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* wherever we find him, and we will descend on him like dew on the ground. And of all the men with him, not even one will remain. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 If he retreats to a city, all Israel will bring ropes to that city, and we will drag it down to the valley until not even a pebble can be found there.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* and all the men of Israel said, “The advice of \zwd Hushai the Archite|Q5949258\zwd* is better than that of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd*.” For the LORD had purposed to thwart the good counsel of \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* in order to bring disaster on \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\s1 Hushai’s Warning Saves David
\r (Psalms 55:1–23)
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd Hushai|Q5949258\zwd* told \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd*, the priests, “This is what \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* has advised \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* and the elders of Israel, and this is what I have advised. 
\v 16 Now send quickly and tell \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, ‘Do not spend the night at the fords of the wilderness, but be sure to cross over. Otherwise \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and all the people with him will be swallowed up.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Now \zwd Jonathan|Q20502598\zwd* and \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* were staying at En-rogel, where a servant girl would come and pass along information to them. They in turn would go and inform \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd*, for they dared not be seen entering the city. 
\v 18 But a young man did see them and told \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. So the two left quickly and came to the house of a man in Bahurim. He had a well in his courtyard, and they climbed down into it. 
\v 19 Then the man’s wife took a covering, spread it over the mouth of the well, and scattered grain over it so nobody would know a thing. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 When \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s servants came to the woman at the house, they asked, “Where are \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|Q20502598\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “They have crossed over the brook,” she replied. The men searched but did not find them, so they returned to Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 21 After the men had gone, \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan|Q20502598\zwd* climbed up out of the well and went to inform \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd*, saying, “Get up and cross over the river at once, for \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* has given this advice against you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and all the people with him got up and crossed the Jordan. By daybreak, there was no one left who had not crossed the Jordan. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 When \zwd Ahithophel|Q400294\zwd* saw that his advice had not been followed, he saddled his donkey and set out for his house in his hometown. He put his affairs in order and hanged himself. So he died and was buried in \zwd his father|\zwd*’s tomb. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went to Mahanaim, and \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* crossed the Jordan with all the men of Israel. 
\v 25 \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* had appointed \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* over the army in place of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*. \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* was the son of a man named \zwd Ithra|\zwd*,\f + \fr 17:25 \ft Ithra is a variant of Jether; see 1 Kings 2:5.\f* the Ishmaelite \f + \fr 17:25 \ft Some LXX manuscripts (see also 1 Chronicles 2:17); MT and other LXX manuscripts Israelite\f* who had married \zwd Abigail|\zwd*,\f + \fr 17:25 \ft Or Abigal, a variant of Abigail\f* the daughter of \zwd Nahash|\zwd* \f + \fr 17:25 \ft Or of Jesse; see DSS and 1 Chronicles 2:13–16\f* and sister of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd* the mother of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*. 
\v 26 So the Israelites and \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* camped in the land of Gilead. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* came to Mahanaim, he was met by \zwd Shobi son of \zwd Nahash|\zwd*|\zwd* from Rabbah of the Ammonites, \zwd Machir son of \zwd Ammiel|\zwd*|Q18222843\zwd* from Lo-debar, and \zwd Barzillai the Gileadite|\zwd* from Rogelim. 
\v 28 They brought beds, basins, and earthen vessels, as well as wheat, barley, flour, roasted grain, beans, lentils, \f + \fr 17:28 \ft Most LXX manuscripts and Syriac; Hebrew includes roasted seeds.\f* 
\v 29 honey, curds, sheep, and cheese from the herd for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his people to eat. For they said, “The people have become hungry, exhausted, and thirsty in the wilderness.” 
\c 18
\s1 Absalom Killed
\m 
\v 1 Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* reviewed his troops and appointed over them commanders of thousands and of hundreds. 
\v 2 He sent out the troops, a third under \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, a third under \zwd \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s brother Abishai son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1154317\zwd*, and a third under \zwd Ittai the Gittite|Q11714286\zwd*. And \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to the troops, “I will surely march out with you as well.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But the people pleaded, “You must not go out! For if we have to flee, they will not care about us. Even if half of us die, they will not care. But you are worth ten thousand of us.\f + \fr 18:3 \ft Two Hebrew manuscripts, some LXX manuscripts, and Vulgate; most Hebrew manuscripts care; for now there are ten thousand like us\f* It is better now if you support us from the city.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “I will do whatever seems best to you,” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* replied. So he stood beside the gate, while all the troops marched out by hundreds and by thousands. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Now \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* had commanded \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, and \zwd Ittai|Q11714286\zwd*, “Treat the young man \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* gently for my sake.” And all the people heard \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s orders to each of the commanders regarding \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s army marched into the field to engage Israel, and the battle took place in the forest of Ephraim. 
\v 7 There the people of Israel were defeated by \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s servants, and the slaughter was great that day—twenty thousand men. 
\v 8 The battle spread over the whole countryside, and that day the forest devoured more people than the sword. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Now \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* was riding on his mule when he met the servants of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, and as the mule went under the thick branches of a large oak,\f + \fr 18:9 \ft Or large terebinth or very great tree; similarly in verses 10 and 14\f* \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*’s head was caught fast in the tree. The mule under him kept going, so that he was suspended in midair. 
\v 10 When one of the men saw this, he told \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “I just saw \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* hanging in an oak tree!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 “You just saw him!” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* exclaimed. “Why did you not strike him to the ground right there? I would have given you ten shekels of silver \f + \fr 18:11 \ft 10 shekels is approximately 4 ounces or 114 grams of silver.\f* and a warrior’s belt!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 The man replied, “Even if a thousand shekels of silver \f + \fr 18:12 \ft 1,000 shekels is approximately 25.1 pounds or 11.4 kilograms of silver.\f* were weighed out into my hands, I would not raise my hand against \zwd the son of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*|Q205372\zwd*. For we heard \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* command you and \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* and \zwd Ittai|Q11714286\zwd*, ‘Protect \zwd the young man Absalom|Q205372\zwd* for my sake.\f + \fr 18:12 \ft A few Hebrew manuscripts, LXX, Vulgate, and Syriac; most Hebrew manuscripts Whoever you may be, protect the young man Absalom. or Let no one touch the young man Absalom.\f*’ 
\v 13 If I had jeopardized my own life \f + \fr 18:13 \ft Or If I had dealt treacherously against his life\f*—and nothing is hidden from \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*—you would have abandoned me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 But \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* declared, “I am not going to wait like this with you!” And he took three spears in his hand and thrust them through the heart of \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd* while he was still alive in the oak tree. 
\v 15 And ten young men who carried \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s armor surrounded \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, struck him, and killed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* blew the ram’s horn, and the troops broke off their pursuit of Israel because \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* had restrained them. 
\v 17 They took \zwd Absalom|Q205372\zwd*, cast him into a large pit in the forest, and piled a huge mound of stones over him. Meanwhile, all the Israelites fled, each to his home. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 During his lifetime, \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd* had set up for himself a pillar in the King’s Valley, for he had said, “I have no son to preserve the memory of my name.” So he gave the pillar his name, and to this day it is called \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*’s Monument. 
\s1 David Mourns for Absalom
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then \zwd Ahimaaz son of \zwd Zadok|id="Q140100" t="2"\zwd*|Q2904547\zwd* said, “Please let me run and tell \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* the good news that the LORD has avenged him of his enemies.” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 But \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied, “You are not the man to take good news today. You may do it another day, but you must not do so today, because \zwd \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s son|Q205372\zwd* is dead.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* said to a Cushite, “Go, tell \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* what you have seen.” The Cushite bowed to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and took off running. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd Ahimaaz son of \zwd Zadok|id="Q140100" t="2"\zwd*|Q2904547\zwd*, however, persisted and said to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “No matter what, please let me also run behind the Cushite!” 
\b
\m “\zwd My son|Q2904547\zwd*,” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied, “why do you want to run, since you will not receive a reward?” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 “No matter what, I want to run!” he replied. 
\b
\m “Then run!” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* told him. 
\b
\m So \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* ran by way of the plain \f + \fr 18:23 \ft That is, the plain of the Jordan\f* and outran the Cushite. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Now \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was sitting between the two gates when the watchman went up to the roof of the gateway by the wall, looked out, and saw a man running alone. 
\v 25 So he called out and told \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m “If he is alone,” \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* replied, “he bears good news.” 
\b
\m As the first runner drew near, 
\v 26 the watchman saw another man running, and he called out to the gatekeeper, “Look! Another man is running alone!” 
\b
\m “This one also brings good news,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 27 The watchman said, “The first man appears to me to be running like \zwd Ahimaaz son of \zwd Zadok|id="Q140100" t="2"\zwd*|Q2904547\zwd*.” 
\b
\m “This is a good man,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. “He comes with good news.” 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Then \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* called out to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “All is well!” And he bowed facedown before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m He continued, “Blessed be the LORD your God! He has delivered up the men who raised their hands against \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* asked, “Is \zwd the young man Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd* all right?” 
\b
\m And \zwd Ahimaaz|Q2904547\zwd* replied, “When \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* sent \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s servant and your servant, I saw a great tumult, but I do not know what it was.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 “Move aside,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “and stand here.” 
\b
\m So he stepped aside. 
\b
\m 
\v 31 Just then the Cushite came and said, “May \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* hear the good news: Today the LORD has avenged you of all who rose up against you!” 
\b
\m 
\v 32 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* asked the Cushite, “Is \zwd the young man Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd* all right?” 
\b
\m And the Cushite replied, “May the enemies of \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* and all who rise up against you to harm you be like \zwd that young man|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* was shaken and went up to the chamber over the gate and wept. And as he walked, he cried out, “O \zwd my son Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*! My son, \zwd my son Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*! If only I had died instead of you, O \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*, my son, my son!” 
\c 19
\s1 Joab Reproves David
\m 
\v 1 Then it was reported to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, “\zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* is weeping and mourning over \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\v 2 And that day’s victory was turned into mourning for all the people, because on that day they were told, “\zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* is grieving over \zwd his son|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So they returned to the city quietly that day, as people steal away in humiliation after fleeing a battle. 
\v 4 But \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* covered his face and cried out at the top of his voice, “O \zwd my son Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*! O \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*, my son, my son!” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* went into the house and said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Today you have disgraced all your servants who have saved your life and the lives of your sons and daughters, of your wives, and of your concubines. 
\v 6 You love those who hate you and hate those who love you! For you have made it clear today that the commanders and soldiers mean nothing to you. I know today that if \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd* were alive and all of us were dead, it would have pleased you! 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now therefore get up! Go out and speak comfort to your servants, for I swear by the LORD that if you do not go out, not a man will remain with you tonight. This will be worse for you than all the adversity that has befallen you from your youth until now!” 
\s1 David Restored as King
\b
\m 
\v 8 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* got up and sat in the gate, and all the people were told: “Behold, \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* is sitting in the gate.” So they all came before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m Meanwhile, the Israelites had fled, each man to his home. 
\v 9 And all the people throughout the tribes of Israel were arguing, “\zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* rescued us from the hand of our enemies and delivered us from the hand of the Philistines, but now he has fled the land because of \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 10 But \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*, the man we anointed over us, has died in battle. So why do you say nothing about restoring \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 Then \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd* sent this message to \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd*, the priests: “Say to the elders of Judah, ‘Why should you be the last to restore \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* to his palace, since the talk of all Israel has reached \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* at his quarters? 
\v 12 You are my brothers, my own flesh and blood. So why should you be the last to restore \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*?’ 
\v 13 And say to \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd*, ‘Aren’t you my flesh and blood? May God punish me, and ever so severely, if from now on you are not the commander of my army in place of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*!’” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 So he swayed the hearts of all the men of Judah as though they were one man, and they sent word to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*: “Return, you and all your servants.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* returned, and when he arrived at the Jordan, the men of Judah came to Gilgal to meet him and escort him across the Jordan. 
\s1 Shimei Pardoned
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \zwd Shimei son of \zwd Gera|\zwd*|Q6978950\zwd*, a Benjamite from Bahurim, hurried down with the men of Judah to meet \zwd King David|Q41370\zwd*, 
\v 17 along with a thousand men of Benjamin, as well as \zwd Ziba the steward of the house of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|Q8071402\zwd* and his fifteen sons and twenty servants. 
\b
\m They rushed down to the Jordan before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* 
\v 18 and crossed at the ford to carry over \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s household and to do what was good in his sight. 
\b
\m When \zwd Shimei son of \zwd Gera|\zwd*|Q6978950\zwd* crossed the Jordan, he fell down before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* 
\v 19 and said, “\zwd My lord|Q41370\zwd*, do not hold me guilty, and do not remember \zwd your servant|Q6978950\zwd*’s wrongdoing on the day \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* left Jerusalem. May \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* not take it to heart. 
\v 20 For \zwd your servant|Q6978950\zwd* knows that I have sinned, so here I am today as the first of all the house of \zwd Joseph|id="Q145746" t="2"\zwd* to come down to meet \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*.” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 But \zwd Abishai son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1154317\zwd* said, “Shouldn’t \zwd Shimei|Q6978950\zwd* be put to death for this, because he cursed \zwd the LORD’s anointed|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied, “Sons of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*, what have I to do with you, that you should be my adversaries today? Should any man be put to death in Israel today? Am I not indeed aware that today I am king over Israel?” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Shimei|Q6978950\zwd*, “You shall not die.” And \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* swore an oath to him. 
\s1 Mephibosheth Excused
\b
\m 
\v 24 Then \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*, \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s grandson, went down to meet \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. He had not cared for his feet or trimmed his mustache or washed his clothes from the day \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* had left until the day he returned safely. 
\v 25 And he came from Jerusalem to meet \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, who asked him, “\zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*, why did you not go with me?” 
\b
\m 
\v 26 “\zwd My lord the king|Q41370\zwd*,” he replied, “because I am lame, I said, ‘I will have my donkey saddled \f + \fr 19:26 \ft LXX, Syriac, and Vulgate Saddle a donkey for me; Hebrew I will saddle a donkey for myself\f* so that I may ride on it and go with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.’ But \zwd my servant Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* deceived me, 
\v 27 and he has slandered \zwd your servant|Q851686\zwd* to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m Yet \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* is like the angel \f + \fr 19:27 \ft Or Angel\f* of God, so do what is good in your eyes. 
\v 28 For all the house of \zwd my grandfather|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* deserves death from \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, yet you have set \zwd your servant|Q851686\zwd* among those who eat at your table. What further right, then, do I have to keep appealing to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 29 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* replied, “Why say any more? I hereby declare that you and \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* are to divide the land.” 
\b
\m 
\v 30 And \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd* said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “Instead, since \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* has safely come to his own house, let \zwd Ziba|Q8071402\zwd* take it all!” 
\s1 David’s Kindness to Barzillai
\b
\m 
\v 31 Now \zwd Barzillai the Gileadite|Q6710215\zwd* had come down from Rogelim to cross the Jordan with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and send him on his way from there. 
\v 32 \zwd Barzillai|Q6710215\zwd* was quite old, eighty years of age, and since he was a very wealthy man, he had provided for \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* while he stayed in Mahanaim. 
\b
\m 
\v 33 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Barzillai|Q6710215\zwd*, “Cross over with me, and I will provide for you at my side in Jerusalem.” 
\b
\m 
\v 34 But \zwd Barzillai|Q6710215\zwd* replied, “How many years of my life remain, that I should go up to Jerusalem with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*? 
\v 35 I am now eighty years old. Can I discern what is good and what is not? Can your servant taste what he eats or drinks? Can I still hear the voice of singing men and women? Why should \zwd your servant|Q6710215\zwd* be an added burden to \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*? 
\b
\m 
\v 36 \zwd Your servant|Q6710215\zwd* will go with \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* only a short distance past the Jordan; why should \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* repay me with such a reward? 
\v 37 Please let \zwd your servant|Q6710215\zwd* return, that I may die in my own city near the tomb of my father and mother. But here is \zwd your servant Chimham|Q30526936\zwd*. Let him cross over with \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd*, and do for him what is good in your sight.” 
\b
\m 
\v 38 \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* replied, “\zwd Chimham|Q30526936\zwd* will cross over with me, and I will do for him what is good in your sight, and I will do for you whatever you desire of me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 39 So all the people crossed the Jordan, and then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* crossed over. \zwd The king|Q41370\zwd* kissed \zwd Barzillai|Q6710215\zwd* and blessed him, and \zwd Barzillai|Q6710215\zwd* returned home. 
\b
\m 
\v 40 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* crossed over to Gilgal, and \zwd Chimham|Q30526936\zwd* crossed over with him. All the troops of Judah and half the troops of Israel escorted \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\s1 Contention over the King
\b
\m 
\v 41 Soon all the men of Israel came to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and asked, “Why did our brothers, the men of Judah, take you away secretly and bring \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and his household across the Jordan, together with all of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s men?” 
\b
\m 
\v 42 And all the men of Judah replied to the men of Israel, “We did this because \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* is our relative. Why does this anger you? Have we ever eaten at \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s expense or received anything for ourselves?” 
\b
\m 
\v 43 “We have ten shares in \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*,” answered the men of Israel, “so we have more claim to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* than you. Why then do you despise us? Were we not the first to speak of restoring our king?” 
\b
\m But the men of Judah spoke more fiercely than the men of Israel. 
\c 20
\s1 Sheba’s Rebellion
\m 
\v 1 Now a worthless man named \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*, a Benjamite, happened to be there, and he blew the ram’s horn and shouted: 
\b
\q1 “We have no share in \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, 
\q2 no inheritance in \zwd \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*’s son|Q41370\zwd*. 
\q1 Every man to his tent, 
\q2 O Israel!” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So all the men of Israel deserted \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to follow \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*. But the men of Judah stayed by \zwd their king|Q41370\zwd* all the way from the Jordan to Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* returned to his palace in Jerusalem, he took the ten concubines he had left to take care of the palace, and he placed them in a house under guard. He provided for them, but he no longer slept with them. They were confined until the day of their death, living as widows. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Then \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd*, “Summon the men of Judah to come to me within three days, and be here yourself.” 
\b
\m 
\v 5 So \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* went to summon Judah, but he took longer than the time allotted him. 
\b
\m 
\v 6 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, “Now \zwd Sheba the son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd* will do us more harm than \zwd Absalom|id="Q205372" t="2"\zwd*. Take your lord’s servants and pursue him, or he will find fortified cities and elude us.\f + \fr 20:6 \ft Or and snatch away our eyes or and do us serious harm\f*” 
\b
\m 
\v 7 So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s men, along with the Cherethites, the Pelethites, and all the mighty men, marched out of Jerusalem in pursuit of \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*. 
\v 8 And while they were at the great stone in Gibeon, \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* joined them. 
\b
\m Now \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* was dressed in military attire, with a dagger strapped to his belt. And as he stepped forward, he slipped the dagger from its sheath.\f + \fr 20:8 \ft Literally Now Joab was dressed in military attire, and over it was a belt around his waist with a dagger in its sheath. And as he stepped forward, it fell out.\f* 
\v 9 “Are you well, \zwd my brother|Q2689456\zwd*?” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* asked \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd*. And with his right hand \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* grabbed \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* by the beard to kiss him. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* was not on guard against the dagger in \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s hand, and \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* stabbed him in the stomach and spilled out his intestines on the ground. And \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* did not need to strike him again, for \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* was dead. Then \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and \zwd his brother Abishai|Q1154317\zwd* pursued \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 One of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s young men stood near \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* and said, “Whoever favors \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*, and whoever is for \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, let him follow \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*!” 
\v 12 But \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd* wallowed in his blood in the middle of the road, and when the man saw that all the troops were stopping there, he dragged the body off the road into a field and threw a garment over it. 
\v 13 As soon as \zwd Amasa|Q2689456\zwd*’s body was removed from the road, all the men went on with \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* to pursue \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zwd Sheba|Q2627537\zwd* passed through all the tribes of Israel to Abel-beth-maacah \f + \fr 20:14 \ft Hebrew to Abel and Beth-maacah; see verse 15.\f* and through the entire region of the Berites,\f + \fr 20:14 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate Bicrites\f* who gathered together and followed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 And \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*’s troops came and besieged \zwd Sheba|Q2627537\zwd* in Abel-beth-maacah and built a siege ramp against the outer rampart of the city. 
\b
\m As all the troops with \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* were battering the wall to topple it, 
\v 16 a wise woman called out from the city, “Listen! Listen! Please tell \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* to come here so that I may speak with him.” 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When he had come near to her, the woman asked, “Are you \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*?” 
\b
\m “I am,” he replied. 
\b
\m “Listen to the words of your servant,” she said. 
\b
\m “I am listening,” he answered. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Then the woman said, “Long ago they used to say, ‘Seek counsel at Abel,’ and that is how disputes were settled. 
\v 19 I am among the peaceable and faithful in Israel, but you are trying to destroy a city that is a mother in Israel. Why would you swallow up the LORD’s inheritance?” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 “Far be it!” \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* declared. “Far be it from me to swallow up or destroy! 
\v 21 That is not the case. But a man named \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd*, from the hill country of Ephraim, has lifted up his hand against \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, against \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. Deliver him alone, and I will depart from the city.” 
\b
\m “Look,” the woman replied, “his head will be thrown to you over the wall.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 Then the woman went to all the people with her wise counsel, and they cut off the head of \zwd Sheba son of \zwd Bichri|id="Q106079707" t="2"\zwd*|Q2627537\zwd* and threw it to \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd*. So he blew the ram’s horn and his men dispersed from the city, each to his own home. And \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* returned to the king in Jerusalem. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Now \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* was over the whole army of Israel; \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* was over the Cherethites and Pelethites; 
\v 24 \zwd Adoram|Q4057937\zwd*\f + \fr 20:24 \ft Adoram is a variant of Adoniram and Hadoram; see 1 Kings 4:6 and 2 Chronicles 10:18.\f* was in charge of the forced labor; \zwd Jehoshaphat son of \zwd Ahilud|id="Q132156345" t="2"\zwd*|Q20502574\zwd* was the recorder; 
\v 25 \zwd Sheva|Q124385844\zwd* \f + \fr 20:25 \ft Sheva is also called Seraiah, Shisha, and Shavsha; see 2 Samuel 8:17, 1 Kings 4:3, and 1 Chronicles 18:16.\f* was the scribe; \zwd Zadok|Q140100\zwd* and \zwd Abiathar|Q321804\zwd* were priests; 
\v 26 and \zwd Ira the Jairite|\zwd* \f + \fr 20:26 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts and Syriac Ithrite; see 2 Samuel 23:38.\f* was \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s priest. 
\c 21
\s1 David Avenges the Gibeonites
\m 
\v 1 During the reign of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* there was a famine for three successive years, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sought the face of the LORD. 
\b
\m And the LORD said, “It is because of the blood shed by \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and his family, because he killed the Gibeonites.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 At this, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* summoned the Gibeonites and spoke to them. (Now the Gibeonites were not Israelites, but a remnant of the Amorites. The Israelites had taken an oath concerning them, but in his zeal for Israel and Judah, \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* had sought to kill them.) 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* asked the Gibeonites, “What shall I do for you? How can I make amends so that you may bless the inheritance of the LORD?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 The Gibeonites said to him, “We need no silver or gold from \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* or his house, nor should you put to death anyone in Israel for us.” 
\b
\m “Whatever you ask, I will do for you,” he replied. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 And they answered \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “As for the man who consumed us and plotted against us to exterminate us from existing within any border of Israel, 
\v 6 let seven of his male descendants be delivered to us so that we may hang them \f + \fr 21:6 \ft Or expose them or execute them; similarly in verse 9\f* before the LORD at Gibeah of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, the chosen of the LORD.” 
\b
\m “I will give them to you,” said \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 Now \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* spared \zwd Mephibosheth son of \zwd Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*|Q851686\zwd*, the son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, because of the oath before the LORD between \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and \zwd Jonathan son of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 8 But \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* took \zwd Armoni|Q40326679\zwd* and \zwd Mephibosheth|Q851686\zwd*, the two sons whom \zwd Rizpah daughter of \zwd Aiah|id="Q132154410" t="2"\zwd*|Q2899077\zwd* had borne to \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*, as well as \zwd the five sons whom \zwd Merab \f + \fr 21:8 \ft Two Hebrew manuscripts, some LXX manuscripts, and Syriac (see also 1 Samuel 18:19); most Hebrew and LXX manuscripts Michal\f* daughter of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd* had borne to \zwd Adriel son of \zwd Barzillai the Meholathite|id="Q6710215" t="2"\zwd*|Q2825091\zwd*|\zwd*. 
\v 9 And he delivered them into the hands of the Gibeonites, and they hanged them on the hill before the LORD. So all seven of them fell together; they were put to death in the first days of the harvest, at the beginning of the barley harvest. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 And \zwd Rizpah the daughter of \zwd Aiah|id="Q132154410" t="2"\zwd*|Q2899077\zwd* took sackcloth and spread it out for herself on a rock. From the beginning of the harvest until the rain from heaven poured down on the bodies, she did not allow the birds of the air to rest on them by day, nor the beasts of the field by night. 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was told what \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s concubine Rizpah|Q2899077\zwd*, daughter of \zwd Aiah|id="Q132154410" t="2"\zwd*, had done, 
\v 12 he went and took the bones of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* from the men of Jabesh-gilead, who had stolen them from the public square of Beth-shan where the Philistines had hung the bodies after they had struck down \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* at Gilboa. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had the bones of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* brought from there, and they also gathered the bones of those who had been hanged. 
\v 14 And they buried the bones of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd* and \zwd his son Jonathan|id="Q74891" t="2"\zwd* in Zela in the land of Benjamin, in the tomb of \zwd \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*’s father Kish|id="Q2915270" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m After they had done everything \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* had commanded, God answered their prayers for the land. 
\s1 Four Battles against the Philistines
\r (1 Chronicles 20:4–8)
\b
\m 
\v 15 Once again the Philistines waged war against Israel, and \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his servants went down and fought against the Philistines. But \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* became exhausted. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Then \zwd Ishbi-benob|Q120758253\zwd*, a descendant of \zwd Rapha|id="Q2131345" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 21:16 \ft Or the giant; also in verses 18, 20, and 22\f* whose bronze spear weighed three hundred shekels \f + \fr 21:16 \ft 300 shekels is approximately 7.5 pounds or 3.4 kilograms.\f* and who was bearing a new sword, resolved to kill \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 17 But \zwd Abishai son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*|Q1154317\zwd* came to his aid, struck the Philistine, and killed him. 
\b
\m Then \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s men swore to him, “You must never again go out with us to battle, so that the lamp of Israel may not be extinguished.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Some time later at Gob, there was another battle with the Philistines. At that time \zwd Sibbecai the Hushathite|Q133268748\zwd* killed \zwd Saph|Q140420066\zwd*,\f + \fr 21:18 \ft Saph is a variant of Sippai; see 1 Chronicles 20:4.\f* a descendant of \zwd Rapha|id="Q2131345" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Once again there was a battle with the Philistines at Gob, and \zwd Elhanan son of Jair|Q5360135\zwd* \f + \fr 21:19 \ft See 1 Chronicles 20:5; Hebrew Jaare-oregim\f* the Bethlehemite killed \zwd the brother of \zwd Goliath \f + \fr 21:19 \ft See 1 Chronicles 20:5; Hebrew does not include the brother of.\f* the Gittite|id="Q192785" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, the shaft of whose spear was like a weaver’s beam. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And there was also a battle at Gath, where there was a man of great stature with six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot—twenty-four in all. He too was descended from \zwd Rapha|id="Q2131345" t="2"\zwd*, 
\v 21 and when he taunted Israel, \zwd Jonathan the son of \zwd \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s brother Shimei|id="Q16130530" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd* \f + \fr 21:21 \ft Shimei is a variant of Shammah, Shimeah, and Shimea; see 1 Samuel 16:9, 2 Samuel 13:3, and 1 Chronicles 2:13.\f* killed him. 
\b
\m 
\v 22 So \zwd these four descendants of \zwd Rapha|id="Q2131345" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd* in Gath fell at the hands of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his servants. 
\c 22
\s1 David’s Song of Deliverance
\r (Psalms 18:1–50)
\m 
\v 1 And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* sang this song to the LORD on the day the LORD had delivered him from the hand of all his enemies and from the hand of \zwd Saul|id="Q28730" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 2 He said: 
\b
\q1 “The LORD is my rock, 
\q2 my fortress, and my deliverer. 
\q1 
\v 3 My God is my rock, in whom I take refuge, 
\q2 my shield, and the horn of my salvation. 
\q1 My stronghold, my refuge, and my Savior, 
\q2 You save me from violence. 
\q1 
\v 4 I will call upon the LORD, who is worthy to be praised; 
\q2 so shall I be saved from my enemies. 
\b
\q1 
\v 5 For the waves of death engulfed me; 
\q2 the torrents of chaos overwhelmed me. 
\q1 
\v 6 The cords of Sheol entangled me; 
\q2 the snares of death confronted me. 
\q1 
\v 7 In my distress I called upon the LORD; 
\q2 I cried out to my God. 
\q1 And from His temple He heard my voice, 
\q2 and my cry for help reached His ears. 
\b
\q1 
\v 8 Then the earth shook and quaked; 
\q2 the foundations of the heavens \f + \fr 22:8 \ft Most Hebrew sources; Vulgate, Syriac, and two Hebrew manuscripts (see also Psalms 18:7) mountains\f* trembled; 
\q2 they were shaken because He burned with anger. 
\q1 
\v 9 Smoke rose from His nostrils, 
\q2 and consuming fire came from His mouth; 
\q2 glowing coals blazed forth. 
\b
\q1 
\v 10 He parted the heavens and came down 
\q2 with dark clouds beneath His feet. 
\q1 
\v 11 He mounted a cherub and flew; 
\q2 He soared \f + \fr 22:11 \ft Many Hebrew manuscripts (see also Psalms 18:10); most Hebrew manuscripts He was seen\f* on the wings of the wind. 
\q1 
\v 12 He made darkness a canopy around Him, 
\q2 a gathering of water and thick clouds. 
\q1 
\v 13 From the brightness of His presence 
\q2 coals of fire \f + \fr 22:13 \ft Or bolts of lightning\f* blazed forth. 
\b
\q1 
\v 14 The LORD thundered from heaven; 
\q2 the voice of the Most High resounded. 
\q1 
\v 15 He shot His arrows and scattered the foes; 
\q2 He hurled lightning and routed them. 
\q1 
\v 16 The channels of the sea appeared, 
\q2 and the foundations of the world were exposed 
\q1 at the rebuke of the LORD, 
\q2 at the blast of the breath of His nostrils. 
\b
\q1 
\v 17 He reached down from on high and took hold of me; 
\q2 He drew me out of deep waters. 
\q1 
\v 18 He rescued me from my powerful enemy, 
\q2 from foes too mighty for me. 
\q1 
\v 19 They confronted me in my day of calamity, 
\q2 but the LORD was my support. 
\q1 
\v 20 He brought me out into the open; 
\q2 He rescued me because He delighted in me. 
\b
\q1 
\v 21 The LORD has rewarded me according to my righteousness; 
\q2 He has repaid me according to the cleanness of my hands. 
\q1 
\v 22 For I have kept the ways of the LORD 
\q2 and have not wickedly departed from my God. 
\q1 
\v 23 For all His ordinances are before me; 
\q2 I have not disregarded His statutes. 
\q1 
\v 24 And I have been blameless before Him 
\q2 and kept myself from iniquity. 
\q1 
\v 25 So the LORD has repaid me according to my righteousness, 
\q2 according to my cleanness in His sight.\f + \fr 22:25 \ft Hebrew; LXX and Vulgate (see also Psalms 18:24) according to the cleanness of my hands\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 26 To the faithful You show Yourself faithful, 
\q2 to the blameless You show Yourself blameless; 
\q1 
\v 27 to the pure You show Yourself pure, 
\q2 but to the crooked You show Yourself shrewd. 
\q1 
\v 28 You save an afflicted people, 
\q2 but Your eyes are on the haughty to bring them down. 
\b
\q1 
\v 29 For You, O LORD, are my lamp; 
\q2 the LORD lights up my darkness. 
\q1 
\v 30 For in You I can charge an army; 
\q2 with my God I can scale a wall. 
\q1 
\v 31 As for God, His way is perfect; 
\q2 the word of the LORD is flawless. 
\q1 He is a shield to all 
\q2 who take refuge in Him. 
\b
\q1 
\v 32 For who is God besides the LORD? 
\q2 And who is the Rock except our God? 
\q1 
\v 33 God is my strong fortress, 
\q2 and He makes my way clear. 
\q1 
\v 34 He makes my feet like those of a deer 
\q2 and stations me upon the heights. 
\q1 
\v 35 He trains my hands for battle; 
\q2 my arms can bend a bow of bronze. 
\b
\q1 
\v 36 You have given me Your shield of salvation, 
\q2 and Your gentleness exalts me.\f + \fr 22:36 \ft Or and Your help exalts me or and You stoop down to make me great\f* 
\q1 
\v 37 You broaden the path beneath me 
\q2 so that my ankles do not give way. 
\q1 
\v 38 I pursued my enemies and destroyed them; 
\q2 I did not turn back until they were consumed. 
\q1 
\v 39 I devoured and crushed them so they could not rise; 
\q2 they have fallen under my feet. 
\b
\q1 
\v 40 You have armed me with strength for battle; 
\q2 You have subdued my foes beneath me. 
\q1 
\v 41 You have made my enemies retreat before me; 
\q2 I destroyed those who hated me. 
\q1 
\v 42 They looked, but there was no one to save them—
\q2 to the LORD, but He did not answer. 
\q1 
\v 43 I ground them as the dust of the earth; 
\q2 I crushed and trampled them like mud in the streets. 
\b
\q1 
\v 44 You have delivered me from the strife of my people; 
\q2 You have preserved me as the head of nations; 
\q2 a people I had not known shall serve me. 
\q1 
\v 45 Foreigners cower before me; 
\q2 when they hear me, they obey me. 
\q1 
\v 46 Foreigners lose heart 
\q2 and come trembling \f + \fr 22:46 \ft Some LXX manuscripts and Vulgate (see also Psalms 18:45); MT and arm themselves\f* from their strongholds. 
\b
\q1 
\v 47 The LORD lives, and blessed be my Rock! 
\q2 And may God, the Rock of my salvation, be exalted—
\q1 
\v 48 the God who avenges me 
\q2 and brings down nations beneath me, 
\q2 
\v 49 who frees me from my enemies. 
\q1 You exalt me above my foes; 
\q2 You rescue me from violent men. 
\q1 
\v 50 Therefore I will praise You, O LORD, among the nations; 
\q2 I will sing praises to Your name.\f + \fr 22:50 \ft Cited in Romans 15:9\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 51 Great salvation He brings to \zwd His king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\q2 He shows loving devotion to His anointed, 
\q2 to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and his descendants forever.” 
\c 23
\s1 David’s Last Song
\m 
\v 1 These are the last words of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*: 
\b
\q1 “The oracle of \zwd David son of \zwd Jesse|id="Q45090" t="2"\zwd*|Q41370\zwd*, 
\q2 the oracle of \zwd the man raised on high|Q41370\zwd*, 
\q1 the one anointed by the God of \zwd Jacob|id="Q289957" t="2"\zwd*, 
\q2 and the sweet psalmist of Israel:\f + \fr 23:1 \ft Or the hero of the songs of Israel or the favorite of the Strong One of Israel\f* 
\b
\q1 
\v 2 The Spirit of the LORD spoke through me; 
\q2 His word was on my tongue. 
\q1 
\v 3 The God of Israel spoke; 
\q2 the Rock of Israel said to me, 
\q1 ‘He who rules the people with justice, 
\q2 who rules in the fear of God, 
\q1 
\v 4 is like the light of the morning 
\q2 at sunrise of a cloudless dawn, 
\q1 the glistening after the rain 
\q2 on the sprouting grass of the earth.’ 
\b
\q1 
\v 5 Is not my house right with God? 
\q2 For He has established with me 
\q1 an everlasting covenant, 
\q2 ordered and secured in every part. 
\q1 Will He not bring about my full salvation 
\q2 and my every desire? 
\b
\q1 
\v 6 But the worthless are all like thorns raked aside, 
\q2 for they can never be gathered by hand. 
\q1 
\v 7 The man who touches them must be armed with iron 
\q2 or with the shaft of a spear. 
\q1 The fire burns them to ashes 
\q2 in the place where they lie.” 
\s1 David’s Mighty Men
\r (1 Chronicles 11:10–47)
\b
\m 
\v 8 These are the names of \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s mighty men: 
\b
\li1 \zwd Josheb-basshebeth the Tahchemonite|\zwd* \f + \fr 23:8 \ft Tahchemonite is probably a variant of Hachmonite; see 1 Chronicles 11:11.\f* was chief of the Three.\f + \fr 23:8 \ft Or chief among the captains\f* He wielded his spear against \f + \fr 23:8 \ft Some LXX manuscripts (see also 1 Chronicles 11:11); Hebrew He was called Adino the Eznite because of\f* eight hundred men, whom he killed at one time. 
\b
\li1 
\v 9 Next in command was \zwd Eleazar son of \zwd Dodo|\zwd*|Q5354450\zwd* \f + \fr 23:9 \ft Dodo is a variant of Dodai; see 1 Chronicles 27:4.\f* the Ahohite. As one of the three mighty men, he went with \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* to taunt the Philistines who had gathered for battle at Pas-dammim. The men of Israel retreated, 
\v 10 but \zwd Eleazar|Q5354450\zwd* stood his ground and struck the Philistines until his hand grew weary and stuck to his sword. The LORD brought about a great victory that day. Then the troops returned to him, but only to plunder the dead. 
\b
\li1 
\v 11 And after him was \zwd Shammah son of \zwd Agee the Hararite|\zwd*|\zwd*. When the Philistines had banded together near a field full of lentils, Israel’s troops fled from them. 
\v 12 But \zwd Shammah|\zwd* took his stand in the middle of the field, defended it, and struck down the Philistines. So the LORD brought about a great victory. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 At harvest time, three of the thirty chief men went down to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* at the cave of Adullam, while a company of Philistines was encamped in the Valley of Rephaim. 
\v 14 At that time \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* was in the stronghold, and the garrison of the Philistines was at Bethlehem. 
\v 15 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* longed for water and said, “Oh, that someone would get me a drink of water from the well near the gate of Bethlehem!” 
\b
\m 
\v 16 So \zwd the three mighty men|\zwd* broke through the Philistine camp, drew water from the well near the gate of Bethlehem, and brought it back to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*. But he refused to drink it; instead, he poured it out to the LORD, 
\v 17 saying, “Far be it from me, O LORD, to do this! Is this not the blood of the men who risked their lives?” So he refused to drink it. 
\b
\m Such were the exploits of \zwd the three mighty men|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Now \zwd Abishai|Q1154317\zwd*, the brother of \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and son of \zwd Zeruiah|id="Q196771" t="2"\zwd*, was chief of \zwd the Three|\zwd*,\f + \fr 23:18 \ft Most Hebrew manuscripts (see also 1 Chronicles 11:20); two Hebrew manuscripts and Syriac the Thirty\f* and he wielded his spear against three hundred men, killed them, and won a name along with \zwd the Three|\zwd*. 
\v 19 Was he not more honored than \zwd the Three|\zwd*?\f + \fr 23:19 \ft Hebrew; Syriac the Thirty\f* And he became their commander, even though he was not included among \zwd the Three|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 And \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd* was a man of valor \f + \fr 23:20 \ft Or Benaiah son of Jehoiada was the son of Ishhai\f* from Kabzeel, a man of many exploits. He struck down two champions \f + \fr 23:20 \ft Or two sons of Ariel\f* of Moab, and on a snowy day he went down into a pit and killed a lion. 
\v 21 He also struck down an Egyptian, a huge man. Although the Egyptian had a spear in his hand, \zwd Benaiah|Q816667\zwd* went against him with a club, snatched the spear from his hand, and killed the Egyptian with his own spear. 
\v 22 These were the exploits of \zwd Benaiah son of \zwd Jehoiada|id="Q124375125" t="2"\zwd*|Q816667\zwd*, who won a name along with \zwd the three mighty men|\zwd*. 
\v 23 He was most honored among \zwd the Thirty|\zwd*, but he did not become one of \zwd the Three|\zwd*. And \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* appointed him over his guard. 
\b
\m 
\v 24 Now these were members of \zwd the Thirty|\zwd*:\f + \fr 23:24 \ft Literally In the Thirty (were):\f* 
\b
\li1 \zwd Asahel the brother of \zwd Joab|id="Q1141064" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q2915076" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 \zwd Elhanan son of \zwd Dodo of Bethlehem|\zwd*|\zwd*, 
