\b
\li1 
\v 38 \zwd Ira the Ithrite|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 \zwd Gareb the Ithrite|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 39 and \zwd Uriah the Hittite|id="Q71267" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m There were thirty-seven in all. 
\c 24
\s1 David’s Military Census
\r (Exodus 30:11–16; 1 Chronicles 21:1–6)
\m 
\v 1 Again the anger of the LORD burned against Israel, and He stirred up \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* against them, saying, “Go and take a census of Israel and Judah.” 
\b
\m 
\v 2 So \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* said to \zwd Joab the commander of his army|Q1141064\zwd*, who was with him,\f + \fr 24:2 \ft LXX to Joab and the army commanders with him\f* “Go now throughout the tribes of Israel from Dan to Beersheba and register the troops, so that I may know their number.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 But \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* replied to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “May the LORD your God multiply the troops a hundred times over, and may the eyes of \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* see it. But why does \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* want to do such a thing?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Nevertheless, \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*’s word prevailed against \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and against the commanders of the army. So \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* and the commanders of the army departed from the presence of \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* to register the troops of Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 They crossed the Jordan and camped near Aroer, south of the town in the middle of the valley, and proceeded toward Gad and Jazer. 
\v 6 Then they went to Gilead and the land of Tahtim-hodshi,\f + \fr 24:6 \ft Hebrew; some LXX manuscripts to Gilead and to the land of the Hittites\f* and on to Dan-jaan and around to Sidon. 
\v 7 They went toward the fortress of Tyre and all the cities of the Hivites and Canaanites. Finally, they went on to the Negev of Judah,\f + \fr 24:7 \ft Or to the south of Judah\f* to Beersheba. 
\b
\m 
\v 8 At the end of nine months and twenty days, having gone through the whole land, they returned to Jerusalem. 
\v 9 And \zwd Joab|Q1141064\zwd* reported to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* the total number of the troops. In Israel there were 800,000 men of valor who drew the sword, and in Judah there were 500,000. 
\s1 Judgment for David’s Sin
\r (1 Chronicles 21:7–13)
\b
\m 
\v 10 After \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* had numbered the troops, his conscience was stricken and he said to the LORD, “I have sinned greatly in what I have done. Now, O LORD, I beg You to take away the iniquity of \zwd Your servant|Q41370\zwd*, for I have acted very foolishly.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* got up in the morning, the word of the LORD had come to \zwd Gad the prophet|Q2559051\zwd*, \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*’s seer: 
\v 12 “Go and tell \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* that this is what the LORD says: ‘I am offering you three options. Choose one of them, and I will carry it out against you.’” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So \zwd Gad|Q2559051\zwd* went and said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “Do you choose to endure three \f + \fr 24:13 \ft LXX (see also 1 Chronicles 21:12); Hebrew seven\f* years of famine in your land, three months of fleeing the pursuit of your enemies, or three days of plague upon your land? Now then, think it over and decide how I should reply to Him who sent me.” 
\b
\m 
\v 14 \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* answered \zwd Gad|Q2559051\zwd*, “I am deeply distressed. Please, let us fall into the hand of the LORD, for His mercies are great; but do not let me fall into the hands of men.” 
\s1 A Plague on Israel
\r (1 Chronicles 21:14–17)
\b
\m 
\v 15 So the LORD sent a plague upon Israel from that morning until the appointed time, and seventy thousand of the people from Dan to Beersheba died.\f + \fr 24:15 \ft Literally and of the people from Dan to Beersheba, seventy thousand men died\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 16 But when the angel stretched out his hand to destroy Jerusalem, the LORD relented from the calamity and said to the angel who was destroying the people, “Enough! Withdraw your hand now!” At that time the angel of the LORD was by the threshing floor of \zwd Araunah \f + \fr 24:16 \ft Araunah is a variant of Ornan; see 1 Chronicles 21:15 and 2 Chronicles 3:1.\f* the Jebusite|id="Q2910439" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 When \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* saw the angel striking down the people, he said to the LORD, “Surely I, the shepherd,\f + \fr 24:17 \ft DSS and LXX; MT does not include the shepherd.\f* have sinned and acted wickedly. But these sheep, what have they done? Please, let Your hand fall upon me and \zwd my father|\zwd*’s house.” 
\s1 David Builds an Altar
\r (1 Chronicles 21:18–30)
\b
\m 
\v 18 And that day \zwd Gad|Q2559051\zwd* came to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* and said to him, “Go up and build an altar to the LORD on the threshing floor of \zwd Araunah the Jebusite|id="Q2910439" t="2"\zwd*.” 
\v 19 So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* went up at the word of \zwd Gad|Q2559051\zwd*, just as the LORD had commanded. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 When \zwd Araunah|Q2910439\zwd* looked out and saw \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd* and his servants coming toward him, he went out and bowed facedown before \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*. 
\v 21 “Why has \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* come to his servant?” \zwd Araunah|Q2910439\zwd* said. 
\b
\m “To buy your threshing floor,” \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* replied, “that I may build an altar to the LORD, so that the plague upon the people may be halted.” 
\b
\m 
\v 22 \zwd Araunah|Q2910439\zwd* said to \zwd David|Q41370\zwd*, “May \zwd my lord the king|Q41370\zwd* take whatever seems good to him and offer it up. Here are the oxen for a burnt offering and the threshing sledges and ox yokes for the wood. 
\v 23 O king, \zwd Araunah|Q2910439\zwd* gives all these to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*.” He also said to \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “May the LORD your God accept you.” 
\b
\m 
\v 24 “No,” replied \zwd the king|Q41370\zwd*, “I insist on paying a price, for I will not offer to the LORD my God burnt offerings that cost me nothing.” 
\b
\m So \zwd David|Q41370\zwd* bought the threshing floor and the oxen for fifty shekels of silver.\f + \fr 24:24 \ft 50 shekels is approximately 1.26 pounds or 569.8 grams of silver.\f* 
\v 25 And there he built an altar to the LORD and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings. 
\b
\m Then the LORD answered the prayers on behalf of the land, and the plague upon Israel was halted. 
